entary_en":"These psalms cover a range of human experience before God. Use the chapter summaries as a guide for prayer and reflection.\n\nPsalms 26\nPsalm 26 expresses worship, lament, or wisdom before God. Read it as prayer — many psalms foreshadow Christ and speak to every season of the soul. Read Psalms 26 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nPray the psalm back to God\nThe text of Psalms 26 records or illustrates: Pray the psalm back to God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNotice metaphors for God\nThe text of Psalms 26 records or illustrates: Notice metaphors for God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nApply its emotion honestly\nThe text of Psalms 26 records or illustrates: Apply its emotion honestly. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nPsalms 25: Compare with Psalms 25, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1:7: Wisdom books often repeat the theme that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Proverbs 1:7). Compare how each psalm or proverb applies that foundation differently.\n\nPsalms 27\nPsalm 27 expresses worship, lament, or wisdom before God. Read it as prayer — many psalms foreshadow Christ and speak to every season of the soul. Read Psalms 27 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nPray the psalm back to God\nThe text of Psalms 27 records or illustrates: Pray the psalm back to God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNotice metaphors for God\nThe text of Psalms 27 records or illustrates: Notice metaphors for God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nApply its emotion honestly\nThe text of Psalms 27 records or illustrates: Apply its emotion honestly. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nPsalms 26: Compare with Psalms 26, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1:7: Wisdom books often repeat the theme that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Proverbs 1:7). Compare how each psalm or proverb applies that foundation differently.\n\nPsalms 28\nPsalm 28 expresses worship, lament, or wisdom before God. Read it as prayer — many psalms foreshadow Christ and speak to every season of the soul. Read Psalms 28 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nPray the psalm back to God\nThe text of Psalms 28 records or illustrates: Pray the psalm back to God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNotice metaphors for God\nThe text of Psalms 28 records or illustrates: Notice metaphors for God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nApply its emotion honestly\nThe text of Psalms 28 records or illustrates: Apply its emotion honestly. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nPsalms 27: Compare with Psalms 27, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1:7: Wisdom books often repeat the theme that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Proverbs 1:7). Compare how each psalm or proverb applies that foundation differently.\n\nPsalms 29\nPsalm 29 expresses worship, lament, or wisdom before God. Read it as prayer — many psalms foreshadow Christ and speak to every season of the soul. Read Psalms 29 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nPray the psalm back to God\nThe text of Psalms 29 records or illustrates: Pray the psalm back to God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNotice metaphors for God\nThe text of Psalms 29 records or illustrates: Notice metaphors for God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nApply its emotion honestly\nThe text of Psalms 29 records or illustrates: Apply its emotion honestly. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nPsalms 28: Compare with Psalms 28, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1:7: Wisdom books often repeat the theme that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Proverbs 1:7). Compare how each psalm or proverb applies that foundation differently.\n\nPsalms 30\nPsalm 30 expresses worship, lament, or wisdom before God. Read it as prayer — many psalms foreshadow Christ and speak to every season of the soul. Read Psalms 30 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nPray the psalm back to God\nThe text of Psalms 30 records or illustrates: Pray the psalm back to God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNotice metaphors for God\nThe text of Psalms 30 records or illustrates: Notice metaphors for God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nApply its emotion honestly\nThe text of Psalms 30 records or illustrates: Apply its emotion honestly. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nPsalms 29: Compare with Psalms 29, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1:7: Wisdom books often repeat the theme that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Proverbs 1:7). Compare how each psalm or proverb applies that foundation differently.","commentary_af":"Hierdie psalms dek ''n reeks menslike ervaring voor God. Gebruik die hoofstuk-opsommings as gids vir gebed en refleksie.\n\nPsalms 26\nPsalm 26 druk aanbidding, klagte of wysheid voor God uit. Lees dit as gebed — baie psalms wys na Christus en spreek tot elke seisoen van die siel. Lees Psalms 26 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nBid die psalm terug aan God\nDie teks van Psalms 26 noem of illustreer: Bid die psalm terug aan God. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet op metafore vir God\nDie teks van Psalms 26 noem of illustreer: Let op metafore vir God. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nPas die emosie eerlik toe\nDie teks van Psalms 26 noem of illustreer: Pas die emosie eerlik toe. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet weet dat ware wysheid begin met eerbied vir Hom en daaglikse keuses, spraak en aanbidding vorm.\n\nWhy:\nWysheid verbind insig met die vrees vir die Here. Let op gebooie, gebede of raad in hierdie hoofstuk en watter houding teenoor God dit veronderstel.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nPsalms 25: Compare with Psalms 25, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nSpreuke 1:7: Wysheidsboeke herhaal dat die vrees vir die Here die begin van wysheid is (Spr 1:7). Vergelyk hoe elke psalm of spreuk dit toepas.\n\nPsalms 27\nPsalm 27 druk aanbidding, klagte of wysheid voor God uit. Lees dit as gebed — baie psalms wys na Christus en spreek tot elke seisoen van die siel. Lees Psalms 27 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nBid die psalm terug aan God\nDie teks van Psalms 27 noem of illustreer: Bid die psalm terug aan God. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet op metafore vir God\nDie teks van Psalms 27 noem of illustreer: Let op metafore vir God. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nPas die emosie eerlik toe\nDie teks van Psalms 27 noem of illustreer: Pas die emosie eerlik toe. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet weet dat ware wysheid begin met eerbied vir Hom en daaglikse keuses, spraak en aanbidding vorm.\n\nWhy:\nWysheid verbind insig met die vrees vir die Here. Let op gebooie, gebede of raad in hierdie hoofstuk en watter houding teenoor God dit veronderstel.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nPsalms 26: Compare with Psalms 26, wat direct voor 