INSERT INTO reading_plans (
    slug, title_en, title_af, description_en, description_af,
    study_guide_en, study_guide_af,
    duration_days, category, readings, cover_emoji, sort_order, is_active
) VALUES (
    'bible-365',
    'Bible in a Year',
    'Bybel in ''n Jaar',
    'Read the entire Bible in 365 days — about 3 chapters per day in canonical order.',
    'Lees die hele Bybel in 365 dae — ongeveer 3 hoofstukke per dag in kanonieke volgorde.',
    'Read the entire Bible in one year. Each day lists every chapter with its own summary, highlights, and study questions.',
    'Lees die hele Bybel in een jaar. Elke dag lys elke hoofstuk met sy eie opsomming, sleutel punte en studie vrae.',
    365,
    'whole_bible',
    '[{"day":1,"title_en":"Genesis 1–4","title_af":"Genesis 1–4","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":1},{"book_id":1,"chapter":2},{"book_id":1,"chapter":3},{"book_id":1,"chapter":4}],"commentary_intro_en":"You are reading through the whole Bible. Each chapter summary below helps you grasp the main story before you read the verses.","commentary_intro_af":"Jy lees deur die hele Bybel. Elke hoofstuk-opsomming hieronder help jou die hoofverhaal te begryp voordat jy die verse lees.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":1,"heading_en":"Genesis 1","heading_af":"Genesis 1","summary_en":"The chapter records God creating the heavens and the earth (v. 1). The earth is first described as formless and empty with darkness over the deep; the Spirit of God moves over the waters (v. 2). Over six days God speaks and creation follows: light separated from darkness (vv. 3–5); sky separating waters (vv. 6–8); dry land, seas, and vegetation (vv. 9–13); sun, moon, and stars (vv. 14–19); sea creatures and birds (vv. 20–23); livestock, creeping things, wild animals, and mankind — male and female — in his image (vv. 24–27). God calls each stage good and mankind very good (v. 31). The account of seventh-day rest continues at the start of chapter 2 (2:2–3).","summary_af":"Die hoofstuk beskryf dat God die hemel en die aarde geskape het (v. 1). Die aarde is eers formlos en leeg met duisternis oor die watervloed; die Gees van God beweeg oor die waters (v. 2). Oor ses dae spreek God en skepping volg: lig (vv. 3–5); hemelruim (vv. 6–8); droë land, see en plante (vv. 9–13); son, maan en sterre (vv. 14–19); seediere en voëls (vv. 20–23); vee, kruipende diere, wilde diere en die mens — man en vrou — na sy beeld (vv. 24–27). God noem elke stap goed en die mens baie goed (v. 31). Op die sewende dag rus God, seën die dag en heilig dit (2:2–3).","study_points_en":[{"title":"Creation by God''s spoken command","verses":"vv. 3, 6, 9, 11, 14, 20, 24, 26","detail":"The text repeatedly records God saying \"Let there be…\" or \"Let the earth bring forth…\" and the item appearing. Light (v. 3), the expanse (v. 6), gathered seas and dry land (v. 9), plants (v. 11), heavenly lights (v. 14), sea life and birds (v. 20), land animals (v. 24), and humans (v. 26) each follow a divine word."},{"title":"God declares creation \"good\"","verses":"vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31","detail":"After major stages God sees that it is good (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25). On the sixth day he sees all he made and calls it very good (v. 31). The chapter does not define the word \"good\" but repeats the evaluation at each step."},{"title":"Humanity in God''s image, male and female","verses":"vv. 26–28","detail":"God says \"Let us make man in our image, after our likeness\" (v. 26). They are to be fruitful, multiply, fill the earth, and subdue it; they receive dominion over fish, birds, livestock, and the earth (vv. 26–28). Male and female are both created in his image (v. 27)."},{"title":"Seventh-day rest","verses":"vv. 31–2:3","detail":"After six days of making, God rests on the seventh day from all his work (2:2). He blesses the seventh day and makes it holy because he rested (2:3). The chapter ends with this rest, not with more creation."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Skepping deur God se gesproke bevel","verses":"vv. 3, 6, 9, 11, 14, 20, 24, 26","detail":"Die teks beskryf herhaaldelik dat God sê \"Laat daar wees…\" en dit verskyn: lig (v. 3), uitspansel (v. 6), land en see (v. 9), plante (v. 11), hemelliggame (v. 14), seediere en voëls (v. 20), landdiere (v. 24), en mense (v. 26)."},{"title":"God verklaar die skepping \"goed\"","verses":"vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31","detail":"Na groot stadia sien God dat dit goed is (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25). Op die sesde dag sien hy alles en noem dit baie goed (v. 31)."},{"title":"Mens na God se beeld, man en vrou","verses":"vv. 26–28","detail":"God sê: \"Laat ons mens maak na ons beeld\" (v. 26). Hulle moet vrugbaar wees, vermeerder, die aarde vul en onderwerping uitoefen (vv. 26–28). Man en vrou is beide na sy beeld geskape (v. 27)."},{"title":"Rus op die sewende dag","verses":"vv. 31–2:3","detail":"Na ses dae rus God op die sewende dag van al sy werk (2:2). Hy seën die dag en heilig dit omdat Hy gerus het (2:3)."}],"teaching_en":"God wants us to know that he alone is the Creator of all things, that what he made is good, and that human beings occupy a unique place as his image-bearers under his rule.","teaching_why_en":"The chapter records no other source of existence except God''s word (vv. 3–26). He repeatedly evaluates creation as \"good\" (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25) and mankind as \"very good\" (v. 31). Humanity receives dominion—not independence from God (vv. 26–28).","teaching_af":"God wil hê ons moet weet dat Hy alleen die Skepper van alle dinge is, dat wat Hy gemaak het goed is, en dat mense ''n unieke plek inneem as sy beeld-draers onder sy heerskappy.","teaching_why_af":"Die hoofstuk beskryf geen andere bron van bestaan as God se woord nie (vv. 3–26). Hy beoordeel herhaaldelik die skepping as \"goed\" (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25) en die mens as \"baie goed\" (v. 31). Die mens ontvang heerskappy—nie onafhanklikheid van God nie (vv. 26–28).","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"John 1:1–3","comparison":"Genesis 1 records God creating by spoken command over six days. John 1 records that \"all things were made through\" the Word who was with God in the beginning. Both tie creation to God''s word — one as narrative, one as prologue."},{"ref":"Psalm 104:1–9","comparison":"Psalm 104 praises God for stretching out the heavens, setting the earth on its foundations, and bounding the waters — themes that parallel Genesis 1:6–10 where God separates waters and forms dry land."},{"ref":"Exodus 20:11","comparison":"The Sabbath command in Exodus 20:11 points back to Genesis 1, stating that in six days the Lord made heaven, earth, and sea and rested the seventh day — the same six-plus-one pattern Genesis 1 narrates."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Johannes 1:1–3","comparison":"Genesis 1 beskryf God wat deur gesproke bevel oor ses dae skep. Johannes 1 sê dat \"alle dinge deur die Woord\" gemaak is wat in die begin by God was. Beide verbind skepping met God se woord."},{"ref":"Psalm 104:1–9","comparison":"Psalm 104 prys God wat die hemel uitsprei, die aarde op fondamente stel en waters begrens — temas wat ooreenstem met Genesis 1:6–10 waar God waters skei en droë grond vorm."},{"ref":"Exodus 20:11","comparison":"Die Sabbatgebod in Exodus 20:11 verwys terug na Genesis 1 en sê dat die Here in ses dae hemel, aarde en see gemaak het en die sewende dag gerus het — dieselfde ses-plus-een patroon as Genesis 1."}],"highlights_en":["Creation by God''s spoken command","God declares creation \"good\"","Humanity in God''s image, male and female","Seventh-day rest"],"highlights_af":["Skepping deur God se gesproke bevel","God verklaar die skepping \"goed\"","Mens na God se beeld, man en vrou","Rus op die sewende dag"]},{"book_id":1,"chapter":2,"heading_en":"Genesis 2","heading_af":"Genesis 2","summary_en":"The heavens and earth are finished (v. 1). On the seventh day God rests and blesses the day (vv. 2–3). The narrative shifts to when God forms the man from the dust of the ground and breathes into his nostrils the breath of life (v. 7). God plants Eden in the east and places the man there (v. 8). Two trees are named: the tree of life and the tree of knowledge of good and evil (v. 9). A river flows from Eden to water the garden and divides into four rivers named Pishon, Gihon, Tigris, and Euphrates (vv. 10–14). God commands the man not to eat from the tree of knowledge of good and evil, warning of death (vv. 16–17). Animals are formed and named by the man; no helper is found (vv. 19–20). God builds the woman from the man''s rib; the man calls her \"woman\" and the text states a man leaves father and mother and holds fast to his wife, becoming one flesh (vv. 21–24). They are naked and not ashamed (v. 25).","summary_af":"Die hemel en aarde is voltooi (v. 1). Op die sewende dag rus God (vv. 2–3). Die Here God vorm die man uit stof en blaas lewensasem in sy neus (v. 7). Hy plant Eden in die ooste (v. 8). Twee bome word genoem: lewensboom en boom van kennis (v. 9). ''n Rivier vloei uit Eden en verdeel in vier riviere (vv. 10–14). God verbied die man om van die kennisboom te eet — sterwe sou volg (vv. 16–17). Diere word gevorm en deur die man genoem (vv. 19–20). God bou die vrou uit die man se rib (vv. 21–24). Hulle is naak en nie beskaam nie (v. 25).","study_points_en":[{"title":"Man formed and given breath","verses":"v. 7","detail":"The Lord God forms the man from dust of the ground and breathes into his nostrils the breath of life; the man becomes a living creature. The chapter records both material (dust) and divine breath."},{"title":"Eden and the two named trees","verses":"vv. 8–9, 16–17","detail":"God plants a garden in Eden and puts the man there. The tree of life and the tree of knowledge of good and evil are named. God permits eating from any tree except the tree of knowledge; eating it brings death (vv. 16–17)."},{"title":"Woman formed; one-flesh union stated","verses":"vv. 21–24","detail":"God builds a woman from the man''s rib. The man says she is bone of his bones and flesh of his flesh (v. 23). Verse 24 states a man shall leave father and mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh."},{"title":"Naked without shame","verses":"v. 25","detail":"The chapter ends stating the man and his wife were both naked and were not ashamed — the last recorded condition before chapter 3."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Man gevorm en lewensasem gegee","verses":"v. 7","detail":"Die Here God vorm die man uit stof van die grond en blaas lewensasem in sy neus; die man word ''n lewende wese."},{"title":"Eden en die twee bome","verses":"vv. 8–9, 16–17","detail":"God plant ''n tuin in Eden. Die lewensboom en kennisboom word genoem. Eet van die kennisboom bring die dood (vv. 16–17)."},{"title":"Vrou gevorm; een-vlees unie","verses":"vv. 21–24","detail":"God bou ''n vrou uit die man se rib. V. 24 sê ''n man sal sy vader en moeder verlaat en sy vrou aankleef, en hulle word een vlees."},{"title":"Naak sonder skaamte","verses":"v. 25","detail":"Die hoofstuk eindig: hulle was beide naak en nie beskaam nie — die laaste toestand voor hoofstuk 3."}],"teaching_en":"God wants us to know that life, marriage, and work are gifts placed within boundaries that protect fellowship with him.","teaching_why_en":"God forms the man, gives breath (v. 7), provides Eden (v. 8), sets one clear limit on the tree of knowledge (vv. 16–17), and institutes marriage (vv. 22–24). The man''s work of naming and keeping the garden (vv. 15, 19–20) happens before the fall.","teaching_af":"God wil hê ons moet weet dat lewe, huwelik en werk geskenke is binne grense wat gemeenskap met Hom beskerm.","teaching_why_af":"God vorm die man, gee asem (v. 7), voorsien Eden (v. 8), stel een duidelike grens aan die kennisboom (vv. 16–17), en stel huwelik in (vv. 22–24). Die man se werk om te noem en te bewaar (vv. 15, 19–20) gebeur voor die val.","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"Genesis 1:26–27","comparison":"Genesis 1 gives the wide creation account; Genesis 2 focuses on the man formed from dust (v. 7), Eden planted (v. 8), and the woman formed (v. 22). Both describe humanity''s origin but at different narrative zoom levels."},{"ref":"Revelation 22:1–2","comparison":"Genesis 2 describes a river watering Eden and the tree of life. Revelation 22 again mentions the river of life and the tree of life — language that echoes Eden without repeating Genesis 2''s exact details."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Genesis 1:26–27","comparison":"Genesis 1 gee die breë skeppingsverhaal; Genesis 2 fokus op die man uit stof (v. 7), Eden (v. 8) en die vrou (v. 22). Beide beskryf die mens se oorsprong op verskillende narratiewe vlakke."},{"ref":"Openbaring 22:1–2","comparison":"Genesis 2 beskryf ''n rivier wat Eden besproei en die lewensboom. Openbaring 22 noem weer die rivier van lewe en die lewensboom — taal wat Eden herroep."}],"highlights_en":["Man formed and given breath","Eden and the two named trees","Woman formed; one-flesh union stated","Naked without shame"],"highlights_af":["Man gevorm en lewensasem gegee","Eden en die twee bome","Vrou gevorm; een-vlees unie","Naak sonder skaamte"]},{"book_id":1,"chapter":3,"heading_en":"Genesis 3","heading_af":"Genesis 3","summary_en":"The serpent questions whether God forbade eating from any tree (v. 1). The woman repeats God''s command about the tree in the middle and adds \"neither shall you touch it\" (vv. 2–3). The serpent denies they will die and says their eyes will open knowing good and evil (vv. 4–5). The woman sees the fruit, takes and eats; she gives to her husband with her and he eats (v. 6). Their eyes open; they know they are naked and sew fig leaves (v. 7). They hide from God among the trees (v. 8). God calls \"Where are you?\" (v. 9). The man says he hid because he was afraid; he blames the woman God gave him (vv. 10–12). The woman blames the serpent (v. 13). God curses the serpent to crawl and eat dust; enmity between serpent and woman''s offspring is declared (vv. 14–15). Pain in childbearing and desire toward husband are stated for the woman; rule of husband is stated (v. 16). Ground is cursed for the man; thorns, toil, and sweat until return to dust; death named (vv. 17–19). Garments of skins are made (v. 21). The man is sent out of Eden lest he eat from the tree of life and live forever; cherubim and flaming sword guard the way (vv. 22–24).","summary_af":"Die slang vra of God enige boom verbied het (v. 1). Die vrou herhaal die gebod en voeg by dat hulle dit nie mag aanraak nie (vv. 2–3). Die slang sê hulle sal nie sterf nie (vv. 4–5). Sy neem en eet; sy gee aan haar man en hy eet (v. 6). Hulle merk hulle naakheid en maak vygeblare (v. 7). Hulle verberg vir God (v. 8). God roep: \"Waar is jy?\" (v. 9). Die man skuld die vrou; die vrou skuld die slang (vv. 12–13). God spreek vonnis uit oor slang, vrou en man (vv. 14–19). Velklere word gemaak (v. 21). Hulle word uit Eden verdryf; cherubim bewaak die pad na die lewensboom (vv. 22–24).","study_points_en":[{"title":"Serpent, fruit eaten, eyes opened","verses":"vv. 1–7","detail":"The serpent challenges God''s command (vv. 1–5). Both eat (v. 6). They know they are naked and make coverings (v. 7). The text records eating before their eyes were \"opened\" in the sense of v. 7."},{"title":"Hiding and blame","verses":"vv. 8–13","detail":"They hide when God walks in the garden (v. 8). The man blames the woman; the woman blames the serpent (vv. 12–13). God questions each party before pronouncing consequences."},{"title":"Judgments on serpent, woman, man","verses":"vv. 14–19","detail":"Serpent cursed to crawl (v. 14); pain in childbearing (v. 16); ground cursed, thorns and toil (v. 17); return to dust named (v. 19). Each speech records specific consequences."},{"title":"Expulsion from Eden","verses":"vv. 22–24","detail":"God drives the man out and places cherubim and a flaming sword east of Eden to guard the way to the tree of life (vv. 24). The man is sent to work ground cursed outside Eden."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Slang, vrug geëet, oë geopen","verses":"vv. 1–7","detail":"Die slang betwiste God se gebod (vv. 1–5). Albei eet (v. 6). Hulle merk naakheid en maak bedekking (v. 7)."},{"title":"Wegsteek en skuld","verses":"vv. 8–13","detail":"Hulle verberg wanneer God in die tuin loop (v. 8). Die man skuld die vrou; die vrou die slang (vv. 12–13)."},{"title":"Vonnis op slang, vrou, man","verses":"vv. 14–19","detail":"Slang vervloek (v. 14); pyn in geboorte (v. 16); grond vervloek, dorings en sweet (v. 17); terugkeer na stof (v. 19)."},{"title":"Uitsetting uit Eden","verses":"vv. 22–24","detail":"God dryf die man uit en plaas cherubim met ''n flammende swaard om die pad na die lewensboom te bewaar (v. 24)."}],"teaching_en":"God wants us to know that turning from his word brings real consequences, yet he still speaks judgment and hope in the same breath.","teaching_why_en":"The chapter records disobedience followed by shame, blame, and curse (vv. 7–19). Yet God also announces enmity with the serpent and a future offspring of the woman (v. 15), and he covers the couple before driving them out (vv. 21–24).","teaching_af":"God wil hê ons moet weet dat wegdraai van sy woord werklike gevolge bring, maar dat Hy steeds vonnis en hoop in dieselfde asem spreek.","teaching_why_af":"Die hoofstuk beskryf ongehoorsaamheid gevolg deur skaamte, skuld en vloek (vv. 7–19). Tog kondig God ook vyandskap met die slang en toekomstige nageslag van die vrou aan (v. 15), en bedek die paartjie voor uitsetting (vv. 21–24).","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"2 Corinthians 11:3","comparison":"Genesis 3 records the serpent deceiving the woman. Paul in 2 Corinthians 11:3 refers to Eve being deceived by the serpent''s cunning — the same event cited in later Scripture."},{"ref":"Romans 5:12","comparison":"Genesis 3 records sin entering through disobedience and death named as consequence (v. 19). Romans 5:12 states sin entered the world through one man and death through sin — linking back to this narrative."},{"ref":"Genesis 3:15","comparison":"Within the same chapter, God speaks of enmity between the serpent and the woman''s offspring (v. 15). Later books do not quote this verse often, but the chapter itself pairs judgment with a promise in one speech."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"2 Korintiërs 11:3","comparison":"Genesis 3 beskryf die slang wat die vrou mislei. Paulus in 2 Kor 11:3 verwys na Eva wat deur die slang se sluwenheid mislei is — dieselfde gebeurtenis."},{"ref":"Romeine 5:12","comparison":"Genesis 3 beskryf sonde deur ongehoorsaamheid en dood as gevolg (v. 19). Romeine 5:12 sê sonde het deur een man in die wêreld ingekom en die dood deur die sonde."},{"ref":"Genesis 3:15","comparison":"In dieselfde hoofstuk spreek God van vyandskap tussen die slang en die vrou se nageslag (v. 15). Die hoofstuk self paar vonnis met belofte in een toespraak."}],"highlights_en":["Serpent, fruit eaten, eyes opened","Hiding and blame","Judgments on serpent, woman, man","Expulsion from Eden"],"highlights_af":["Slang, vrug geëet, oë geopen","Wegsteek en skuld","Vonnis op slang, vrou, man","Uitsetting uit Eden"]},{"book_id":1,"chapter":4,"heading_en":"Genesis 4","heading_af":"Genesis 4","summary_en":"The man knows his wife Eve; Cain is born, then Abel (v. 1–2). Abel keeps sheep; Cain works the ground (v. 2). Both bring offerings; God has regard for Abel and his offering but not for Cain''s (vv. 3–5). Cain is angry; God warns that sin crouches at the door and he must rule it (vv. 6–7). Cain rises against Abel and kills him in the field (v. 8). God asks where Abel is; Cain says he does not know, \"Am I my brother''s keeper?\" (vv. 9–10). Abel''s blood cries from the ground; Cain is cursed from the ground and becomes a fugitive (vv. 10–12). Cain receives a mark so no one kills him; he builds a city and names it after his son Enoch (vv. 15–17). Cain''s line lists Lamech who speaks of killing a man (vv. 18–24). Adam and Eve have Seth; people begin calling on the name of the Lord (vv. 25–26).","summary_af":"Die man ken sy vrou Eva; Kain word gebore, dan Abel (vv. 1–2). Abel hou skape; Kain bewerk die grond (v. 2). Beide bring offers; God ag Abel en sy offer maar nie dié van Kain nie (vv. 3–5). Kain is kwaad; God waarsku dat sonde by die deur lê (vv. 6–7). Kain slaan Abel dood in die veld (v. 8). God vra waar Abel is; Kain antwoord hy is nie sy broer se keeper nie (vv. 9–10). Kain is vervloek en kry ''n merk (vv. 11–15). Seth word gebore; mense begin die Naam van die Here aan te roep (vv. 25–26).","study_points_en":[{"title":"Two offerings; Abel accepted, Cain not","verses":"vv. 3–5","detail":"Cain brings fruit of the ground; Abel brings firstborn of flock and fat portions (vv. 3–4). God has regard for Abel and his offering but not Cain''s (v. 5). Cain''s face falls (v. 5)."},{"title":"Murder in the field","verses":"vv. 6–8","detail":"God warns Cain about sin crouching at the door (v. 7). Cain speaks to Abel in the field and kills him when they are alone (v. 8). The text records the first human death by violence."},{"title":"Cain''s curse and mark","verses":"vv. 9–15","detail":"Cain is cursed from the ground that opened its mouth for Abel''s blood (v. 11). He becomes a fugitive (v. 12). God sets a mark on Cain so whoever finds him will not kill him (v. 15)."},{"title":"Seth''s line calls on the Lord","verses":"vv. 25–26","detail":"Eve bears Seth, saying God appointed another offspring (v. 25). At that time people begin calling on the name of the Lord (v. 26). The chapter contrasts Cain''s city line with this statement."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Twee offers; Abel aanvaar, Kain nie","verses":"vv. 3–5","detail":"Kain bring vrug van die grond; Abel bring die firstling van die kudde (vv. 3–4). God ag Abel en sy offer, nie dié van Kain nie (v. 5)."},{"title":"Moord in die veld","verses":"vv. 6–8","detail":"God waarsku Kain dat sonde by die deur lê (v. 7). Kain maak Abel in die veld dood (v. 8)."},{"title":"Kain se vloek en merk","verses":"vv. 9–15","detail":"Kain is vervloek van die grond (v. 11). God merk hom sodat niemand hom sal doodslaan nie (v. 15)."},{"title":"Seth se lyn roep op die Here","verses":"vv. 25–26","detail":"Eva baar Seth (v. 25). Mense begin die Naam van die Here aan te roep (v. 26)."}],"teaching_en":"God wants us to know that he sees injustice, warns before sin escalates, and preserves a line of people who call on him even when violence spreads.","teaching_why_en":"God rejects Cain''s offering and warns him about sin at the door (vv. 5–7). After Abel''s murder God confronts Cain (vv. 9–12) yet marks him for protection (v. 15). The chapter ends with Seth and people calling on the Lord''s name (vv. 25–26).","teaching_af":"God wil hê ons moet weet dat Hy onreg sien, waarsku voordat sonde eskaleer, en ''n lyn mense bewaar wat op Hom roep selfs wanneer geweld versprei.","teaching_why_af":"God verwerp Kain se offer en waarsku hom oor sonde by die deur (vv. 5–7). Na Abel se moord konfronteer God Kain (vv. 9–12) maar merk hom vir beskerming (v. 15). Die hoofstuk eindig met Seth en mense wat die Here se Naam aanroep (vv. 25–26).","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"Hebrews 11:4","comparison":"Genesis 4 records Abel and Cain''s offerings and God regarding Abel''s but not Cain''s. Hebrews 11:4 says by faith Abel offered a more acceptable sacrifice than Cain — referring to the same account."},{"ref":"Matthew 23:35","comparison":"Genesis 4 records Abel''s blood shed by Cain. Jesus in Matthew 23:35 mentions \"righteous Abel\" among those whose blood was shed — naming the same figure Genesis 4 describes."},{"ref":"Genesis 4:7","comparison":"God warns Cain that sin is crouching at the door (v. 7). Compare with later wisdom on choosing obedience: Deuteronomy 30:19 sets life and death before Israel and calls them to choose life."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Hebreërs 11:4","comparison":"Genesis 4 beskryf Abel en Kain se offers en dat God Abel sien maar nie Kain nie. Hebreërs 11:4 sê Abel het deur geloof ''n beter offer gebring as Kain."},{"ref":"Matteus 23:35","comparison":"Genesis 4 beskryf Abel se bloed vergiet deur Kain. Jesus in Matteus 23:35 noem \"regverdige Abel\" onder die wat se bloed vergiet is."},{"ref":"Genesis 4:7","comparison":"God waarsku Kain dat sonde by die deur lê (v. 7. Vergelyk met Deut 30:19 wat lewe en dood voor Israel stel en hulle roep om lewe te kies."}],"highlights_en":["Two offerings; Abel accepted, Cain not","Murder in the field","Cain''s curse and mark","Seth''s line calls on the Lord"],"highlights_af":["Twee offers; Abel aanvaar, Kain nie","Moord in die veld","Kain se vloek en merk","Seth se lyn roep op die Here"]}],"commentary_en":"You are reading through the whole Bible. Each chapter summary below helps you grasp the main story before you read the verses.\n\nGenesis 1\nThe chapter records God creating the heavens and the earth (v. 1). The earth is first described as formless and empty with darkness over the deep; the Spirit of God moves over the waters (v. 2). Over six days God speaks and creation follows: light separated from darkness (vv. 3–5); sky separating waters (vv. 6–8); dry land, seas, and vegetation (vv. 9–13); sun, moon, and stars (vv. 14–19); sea creatures and birds (vv. 20–23); livestock, creeping things, wild animals, and mankind — male and female — in his image (vv. 24–27). God calls each stage good and mankind very good (v. 31). The account of seventh-day rest continues at the start of chapter 2 (2:2–3).\n\nCreation by God''s spoken command [vv. 3, 6, 9, 11, 14, 20, 24, 26]\nThe text repeatedly records God saying \"Let there be…\" or \"Let the earth bring forth…\" and the item appearing. Light (v. 3), the expanse (v. 6), gathered seas and dry land (v. 9), plants (v. 11), heavenly lights (v. 14), sea life and birds (v. 20), land animals (v. 24), and humans (v. 26) each follow a divine word.\n\nGod declares creation \"good\" [vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31]\nAfter major stages God sees that it is good (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25). On the sixth day he sees all he made and calls it very good (v. 31). The chapter does not define the word \"good\" but repeats the evaluation at each step.\n\nHumanity in God''s image, male and female [vv. 26–28]\nGod says \"Let us make man in our image, after our likeness\" (v. 26). They are to be fruitful, multiply, fill the earth, and subdue it; they receive dominion over fish, birds, livestock, and the earth (vv. 26–28). Male and female are both created in his image (v. 27).\n\nSeventh-day rest [vv. 31–2:3]\nAfter six days of making, God rests on the seventh day from all his work (2:2). He blesses the seventh day and makes it holy because he rested (2:3). The chapter ends with this rest, not with more creation.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that he alone is the Creator of all things, that what he made is good, and that human beings occupy a unique place as his image-bearers under his rule.\n\nWhy:\nThe chapter records no other source of existence except God''s word (vv. 3–26). He repeatedly evaluates creation as \"good\" (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25) and mankind as \"very good\" (v. 31). Humanity receives dominion—not independence from God (vv. 26–28).\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nJohn 1:1–3: Genesis 1 records God creating by spoken command over six days. John 1 records that \"all things were made through\" the Word who was with God in the beginning. Both tie creation to God''s word — one as narrative, one as prologue.\nPsalm 104:1–9: Psalm 104 praises God for stretching out the heavens, setting the earth on its foundations, and bounding the waters — themes that parallel Genesis 1:6–10 where God separates waters and forms dry land.\nExodus 20:11: The Sabbath command in Exodus 20:11 points back to Genesis 1, stating that in six days the Lord made heaven, earth, and sea and rested the seventh day — the same six-plus-one pattern Genesis 1 narrates.\n\nGenesis 2\nThe heavens and earth are finished (v. 1). On the seventh day God rests and blesses the day (vv. 2–3). The narrative shifts to when God forms the man from the dust of the ground and breathes into his nostrils the breath of life (v. 7). God plants Eden in the east and places the man there (v. 8). Two trees are named: the tree of life and the tree of knowledge of good and evil (v. 9). A river flows from Eden to water the garden and divides into four rivers named Pishon, Gihon, Tigris, and Euphrates (vv. 10–14). God commands the man not to eat from the tree of knowledge of good and evil, warning of death (vv. 16–17). Animals are formed and named by the man; no helper is found (vv. 19–20). God builds the woman from the man''s rib; the man calls her \"woman\" and the text states a man leaves father and mother and holds fast to his wife, becoming one flesh (vv. 21–24). They are naked and not ashamed (v. 25).\n\nMan formed and given breath [v. 7]\nThe Lord God forms the man from dust of the ground and breathes into his nostrils the breath of life; the man becomes a living creature. The chapter records both material (dust) and divine breath.\n\nEden and the two named trees [vv. 8–9, 16–17]\nGod plants a garden in Eden and puts the man there. The tree of life and the tree of knowledge of good and evil are named. God permits eating from any tree except the tree of knowledge; eating it brings death (vv. 16–17).\n\nWoman formed; one-flesh union stated [vv. 21–24]\nGod builds a woman from the man''s rib. The man says she is bone of his bones and flesh of his flesh (v. 23). Verse 24 states a man shall leave father and mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh.\n\nNaked without shame [v. 25]\nThe chapter ends stating the man and his wife were both naked and were not ashamed — the last recorded condition before chapter 3.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that life, marriage, and work are gifts placed within boundaries that protect fellowship with him.\n\nWhy:\nGod forms the man, gives breath (v. 7), provides Eden (v. 8), sets one clear limit on the tree of knowledge (vv. 16–17), and institutes marriage (vv. 22–24). The man''s work of naming and keeping the garden (vv. 15, 19–20) happens before the fall.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 1:26–27: Genesis 1 gives the wide creation account; Genesis 2 focuses on the man formed from dust (v. 7), Eden planted (v. 8), and the woman formed (v. 22). Both describe humanity''s origin but at different narrative zoom levels.\nRevelation 22:1–2: Genesis 2 describes a river watering Eden and the tree of life. Revelation 22 again mentions the river of life and the tree of life — language that echoes Eden without repeating Genesis 2''s exact details.\n\nGenesis 3\nThe serpent questions whether God forbade eating from any tree (v. 1). The woman repeats God''s command about the tree in the middle and adds \"neither shall you touch it\" (vv. 2–3). The serpent denies they will die and says their eyes will open knowing good and evil (vv. 4–5). The woman sees the fruit, takes and eats; she gives to her husband with her and he eats (v. 6). Their eyes open; they know they are naked and sew fig leaves (v. 7). They hide from God among the trees (v. 8). God calls \"Where are you?\" (v. 9). The man says he hid because he was afraid; he blames the woman God gave him (vv. 10–12). The woman blames the serpent (v. 13). God curses the serpent to crawl and eat dust; enmity between serpent and woman''s offspring is declared (vv. 14–15). Pain in childbearing and desire toward husband are stated for the woman; rule of husband is stated (v. 16). Ground is cursed for the man; thorns, toil, and sweat until return to dust; death named (vv. 17–19). Garments of skins are made (v. 21). The man is sent out of Eden lest he eat from the tree of life and live forever; cherubim and flaming sword guard the way (vv. 22–24).\n\nSerpent, fruit eaten, eyes opened [vv. 1–7]\nThe serpent challenges God''s command (vv. 1–5). Both eat (v. 6). They know they are naked and make coverings (v. 7). The text records eating before their eyes were \"opened\" in the sense of v. 7.\n\nHiding and blame [vv. 8–13]\nThey hide when God walks in the garden (v. 8). The man blames the woman; the woman blames the serpent (vv. 12–13). God questions each party before pronouncing consequences.\n\nJudgments on serpent, woman, man [vv. 14–19]\nSerpent cursed to crawl (v. 14); pain in childbearing (v. 16); ground cursed, thorns and toil (v. 17); return to dust named (v. 19). Each speech records specific consequences.\n\nExpulsion from Eden [vv. 22–24]\nGod drives the man out and places cherubim and a flaming sword east of Eden to guard the way to the tree of life (vv. 24). The man is sent to work ground cursed outside Eden.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that turning from his word brings real consequences, yet he still speaks judgment and hope in the same breath.\n\nWhy:\nThe chapter records disobedience followed by shame, blame, and curse (vv. 7–19). Yet God also announces enmity with the serpent and a future offspring of the woman (v. 15), and he covers the couple before driving them out (vv. 21–24).\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\n2 Corinthians 11:3: Genesis 3 records the serpent deceiving the woman. Paul in 2 Corinthians 11:3 refers to Eve being deceived by the serpent''s cunning — the same event cited in later Scripture.\nRomans 5:12: Genesis 3 records sin entering through disobedience and death named as consequence (v. 19). Romans 5:12 states sin entered the world through one man and death through sin — linking back to this narrative.\nGenesis 3:15: Within the same chapter, God speaks of enmity between the serpent and the woman''s offspring (v. 15). Later books do not quote this verse often, but the chapter itself pairs judgment with a promise in one speech.\n\nGenesis 4\nThe man knows his wife Eve; Cain is born, then Abel (v. 1–2). Abel keeps sheep; Cain works the ground (v. 2). Both bring offerings; God has regard for Abel and his offering but not for Cain''s (vv. 3–5). Cain is angry; God warns that sin crouches at the door and he must rule it (vv. 6–7). Cain rises against Abel and kills him in the field (v. 8). God asks where Abel is; Cain says he does not know, \"Am I my brother''s keeper?\" (vv. 9–10). Abel''s blood cries from the ground; Cain is cursed from the ground and becomes a fugitive (vv. 10–12). Cain receives a mark so no one kills him; he builds a city and names it after his son Enoch (vv. 15–17). Cain''s line lists Lamech who speaks of killing a man (vv. 18–24). Adam and Eve have Seth; people begin calling on the name of the Lord (vv. 25–26).\n\nTwo offerings; Abel accepted, Cain not [vv. 3–5]\nCain brings fruit of the ground; Abel brings firstborn of flock and fat portions (vv. 3–4). God has regard for Abel and his offering but not Cain''s (v. 5). Cain''s face falls (v. 5).\n\nMurder in the field [vv. 6–8]\nGod warns Cain about sin crouching at the door (v. 7). Cain speaks to Abel in the field and kills him when they are alone (v. 8). The text records the first human death by violence.\n\nCain''s curse and mark [vv. 9–15]\nCain is cursed from the ground that opened its mouth for Abel''s blood (v. 11). He becomes a fugitive (v. 12). God sets a mark on Cain so whoever finds him will not kill him (v. 15).\n\nSeth''s line calls on the Lord [vv. 25–26]\nEve bears Seth, saying God appointed another offspring (v. 25). At that time people begin calling on the name of the Lord (v. 26). The chapter contrasts Cain''s city line with this statement.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that he sees injustice, warns before sin escalates, and preserves a line of people who call on him even when violence spreads.\n\nWhy:\nGod rejects Cain''s offering and warns him about sin at the door (vv. 5–7). After Abel''s murder God confronts Cain (vv. 9–12) yet marks him for protection (v. 15). The chapter ends with Seth and people calling on the Lord''s name (vv. 25–26).\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nHebrews 11:4: Genesis 4 records Abel and Cain''s offerings and God regarding Abel''s but not Cain''s. Hebrews 11:4 says by faith Abel offered a more acceptable sacrifice than Cain — referring to the same account.\nMatthew 23:35: Genesis 4 records Abel''s blood shed by Cain. Jesus in Matthew 23:35 mentions \"righteous Abel\" among those whose blood was shed — naming the same figure Genesis 4 describes.\nGenesis 4:7: God warns Cain that sin is crouching at the door (v. 7). Compare with later wisdom on choosing obedience: Deuteronomy 30:19 sets life and death before Israel and calls them to choose life.","commentary_af":"Jy lees deur die hele Bybel. Elke hoofstuk-opsomming hieronder help jou die hoofverhaal te begryp voordat jy die verse lees.\n\nGenesis 1\nDie hoofstuk beskryf dat God die hemel en die aarde geskape het (v. 1). Die aarde is eers formlos en leeg met duisternis oor die watervloed; die Gees van God beweeg oor die waters (v. 2). Oor ses dae spreek God en skepping volg: lig (vv. 3–5); hemelruim (vv. 6–8); droë land, see en plante (vv. 9–13); son, maan en sterre (vv. 14–19); seediere en voëls (vv. 20–23); vee, kruipende diere, wilde diere en die mens — man en vrou — na sy beeld (vv. 24–27). God noem elke stap goed en die mens baie goed (v. 31). Op die sewende dag rus God, seën die dag en heilig dit (2:2–3).\n\nSkepping deur God se gesproke bevel [vv. 3, 6, 9, 11, 14, 20, 24, 26]\nDie teks beskryf herhaaldelik dat God sê \"Laat daar wees…\" en dit verskyn: lig (v. 3), uitspansel (v. 6), land en see (v. 9), plante (v. 11), hemelliggame (v. 14), seediere en voëls (v. 20), landdiere (v. 24), en mense (v. 26).\n\nGod verklaar die skepping \"goed\" [vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31]\nNa groot stadia sien God dat dit goed is (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25). Op die sesde dag sien hy alles en noem dit baie goed (v. 31).\n\nMens na God se beeld, man en vrou [vv. 26–28]\nGod sê: \"Laat ons mens maak na ons beeld\" (v. 26). Hulle moet vrugbaar wees, vermeerder, die aarde vul en onderwerping uitoefen (vv. 26–28). Man en vrou is beide na sy beeld geskape (v. 27).\n\nRus op die sewende dag [vv. 31–2:3]\nNa ses dae rus God op die sewende dag van al sy werk (2:2). Hy seën die dag en heilig dit omdat Hy gerus het (2:3).\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet weet dat Hy alleen die Skepper van alle dinge is, dat wat Hy gemaak het goed is, en dat mense ''n unieke plek inneem as sy beeld-draers onder sy heerskappy.\n\nWhy:\nDie hoofstuk beskryf geen andere bron van bestaan as God se woord nie (vv. 3–26). Hy beoordeel herhaaldelik die skepping as \"goed\" (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25) en die mens as \"baie goed\" (v. 31). Die mens ontvang heerskappy—nie onafhanklikheid van God nie (vv. 26–28).\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nJohannes 1:1–3: Genesis 1 beskryf God wat deur gesproke bevel oor ses dae skep. Johannes 1 sê dat \"alle dinge deur die Woord\" gemaak is wat in die begin by God was. Beide verbind skepping met God se woord.\nPsalm 104:1–9: Psalm 104 prys God wat die hemel uitsprei, die aarde op fondamente stel en waters begrens — temas wat ooreenstem met Genesis 1:6–10 waar God waters skei en droë grond vorm.\nExodus 20:11: Die Sabbatgebod in Exodus 20:11 verwys terug na Genesis 1 en sê dat die Here in ses dae hemel, aarde en see gemaak het en die sewende dag gerus het — dieselfde ses-plus-een patroon as Genesis 1.\n\nGenesis 2\nDie hemel en aarde is voltooi (v. 1). Op die sewende dag rus God (vv. 2–3). Die Here God vorm die man uit stof en blaas lewensasem in sy neus (v. 7). Hy plant Eden in die ooste (v. 8). Twee bome word genoem: lewensboom en boom van kennis (v. 9). ''n Rivier vloei uit Eden en verdeel in vier riviere (vv. 10–14). God verbied die man om van die kennisboom te eet — sterwe sou volg (vv. 16–17). Diere word gevorm en deur die man genoem (vv. 19–20). God bou die vrou uit die man se rib (vv. 21–24). Hulle is naak en nie beskaam nie (v. 25).\n\nMan gevorm en lewensasem gegee [v. 7]\nDie Here God vorm die man uit stof van die grond en blaas lewensasem in sy neus; die man word ''n lewende wese.\n\nEden en die twee bome [vv. 8–9, 16–17]\nGod plant ''n tuin in Eden. Die lewensboom en kennisboom word genoem. Eet van die kennisboom bring die dood (vv. 16–17).\n\nVrou gevorm; een-vlees unie [vv. 21–24]\nGod bou ''n vrou uit die man se rib. V. 24 sê ''n man sal sy vader en moeder verlaat en sy vrou aankleef, en hulle word een vlees.\n\nNaak sonder skaamte [v. 25]\nDie hoofstuk eindig: hulle was beide naak en nie beskaam nie — die laaste toestand voor hoofstuk 3.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet weet dat lewe, huwelik en werk geskenke is binne grense wat gemeenskap met Hom beskerm.\n\nWhy:\nGod vorm die man, gee asem (v. 7), voorsien Eden (v. 8), stel een duidelike grens aan die kennisboom (vv. 16–17), en stel huwelik in (vv. 22–24). Die man se werk om te noem en te bewaar (vv. 15, 19–20) gebeur voor die val.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 1:26–27: Genesis 1 gee die breë skeppingsverhaal; Genesis 2 fokus op die man uit stof (v. 7), Eden (v. 8) en die vrou (v. 22). Beide beskryf die mens se oorsprong op verskillende narratiewe vlakke.\nOpenbaring 22:1–2: Genesis 2 beskryf ''n rivier wat Eden besproei en die lewensboom. Openbaring 22 noem weer die rivier van lewe en die lewensboom — taal wat Eden herroep.\n\nGenesis 3\nDie slang vra of God enige boom verbied het (v. 1). Die vrou herhaal die gebod en voeg by dat hulle dit nie mag aanraak nie (vv. 2–3). Die slang sê hulle sal nie sterf nie (vv. 4–5). Sy neem en eet; sy gee aan haar man en hy eet (v. 6). Hulle merk hulle naakheid en maak vygeblare (v. 7). Hulle verberg vir God (v. 8). God roep: \"Waar is jy?\" (v. 9). Die man skuld die vrou; die vrou skuld die slang (vv. 12–13). God spreek vonnis uit oor slang, vrou en man (vv. 14–19). Velklere word gemaak (v. 21). Hulle word uit Eden verdryf; cherubim bewaak die pad na die lewensboom (vv. 22–24).\n\nSlang, vrug geëet, oë geopen [vv. 1–7]\nDie slang betwiste God se gebod (vv. 1–5). Albei eet (v. 6). Hulle merk naakheid en maak bedekking (v. 7).\n\nWegsteek en skuld [vv. 8–13]\nHulle verberg wanneer God in die tuin loop (v. 8). Die man skuld die vrou; die vrou die slang (vv. 12–13).\n\nVonnis op slang, vrou, man [vv. 14–19]\nSlang vervloek (v. 14); pyn in geboorte (v. 16); grond vervloek, dorings en sweet (v. 17); terugkeer na stof (v. 19).\n\nUitsetting uit Eden [vv. 22–24]\nGod dryf die man uit en plaas cherubim met ''n flammende swaard om die pad na die lewensboom te bewaar (v. 24).\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet weet dat wegdraai van sy woord werklike gevolge bring, maar dat Hy steeds vonnis en hoop in dieselfde asem spreek.\n\nWhy:\nDie hoofstuk beskryf ongehoorsaamheid gevolg deur skaamte, skuld en vloek (vv. 7–19). Tog kondig God ook vyandskap met die slang en toekomstige nageslag van die vrou aan (v. 15), en bedek die paartjie voor uitsetting (vv. 21–24).\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\n2 Korintiërs 11:3: Genesis 3 beskryf die slang wat die vrou mislei. Paulus in 2 Kor 11:3 verwys na Eva wat deur die slang se sluwenheid mislei is — dieselfde gebeurtenis.\nRomeine 5:12: Genesis 3 beskryf sonde deur ongehoorsaamheid en dood as gevolg (v. 19). Romeine 5:12 sê sonde het deur een man in die wêreld ingekom en die dood deur die sonde.\nGenesis 3:15: In dieselfde hoofstuk spreek God van vyandskap tussen die slang en die vrou se nageslag (v. 15). Die hoofstuk self paar vonnis met belofte in een toespraak.\n\nGenesis 4\nDie man ken sy vrou Eva; Kain word gebore, dan Abel (vv. 1–2). Abel hou skape; Kain bewerk die grond (v. 2). Beide bring offers; God ag Abel en sy offer maar nie dié van Kain nie (vv. 3–5). Kain is kwaad; God waarsku dat sonde by die deur lê (vv. 6–7). Kain slaan Abel dood in die veld (v. 8). God vra waar Abel is; Kain antwoord hy is nie sy broer se keeper nie (vv. 9–10). Kain is vervloek en kry ''n merk (vv. 11–15). Seth word gebore; mense begin die Naam van die Here aan te roep (vv. 25–26).\n\nTwee offers; Abel aanvaar, Kain nie [vv. 3–5]\nKain bring vrug van die grond; Abel bring die firstling van die kudde (vv. 3–4). God ag Abel en sy offer, nie dié van Kain nie (v. 5).\n\nMoord in die veld [vv. 6–8]\nGod waarsku Kain dat sonde by die deur lê (v. 7). Kain maak Abel in die veld dood (v. 8).\n\nKain se vloek en merk [vv. 9–15]\nKain is vervloek van die grond (v. 11). God merk hom sodat niemand hom sal doodslaan nie (v. 15).\n\nSeth se lyn roep op die Here [vv. 25–26]\nEva baar Seth (v. 25). Mense begin die Naam van die Here aan te roep (v. 26).\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet weet dat Hy onreg sien, waarsku voordat sonde eskaleer, en ''n lyn mense bewaar wat op Hom roep selfs wanneer geweld versprei.\n\nWhy:\nGod verwerp Kain se offer en waarsku hom oor sonde by die deur (vv. 5–7). Na Abel se moord konfronteer God Kain (vv. 9–12) maar merk hom vir beskerming (v. 15). Die hoofstuk eindig met Seth en mense wat die Here se Naam aanroep (vv. 25–26).\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nHebreërs 11:4: Genesis 4 beskryf Abel en Kain se offers en dat God Abel sien maar nie Kain nie. Hebreërs 11:4 sê Abel het deur geloof ''n beter offer gebring as Kain.\nMatteus 23:35: Genesis 4 beskryf Abel se bloed vergiet deur Kain. Jesus in Matteus 23:35 noem \"regverdige Abel\" onder die wat se bloed vergiet is.\nGenesis 4:7: God waarsku Kain dat sonde by die deur lê (v. 7. Vergelyk met Deut 30:19 wat lewe en dood voor Israel stel en hulle roep om lewe te kies.","questions_en":["What does this passage reveal about God?","What promise, command, or truth stands out to you?","How can you apply this to your life today?"],"questions_af":["Wat openbaar hierdie gedeelte oor God?","Watter belofte, gebod of waarheid val vir jou op?","Hoe kan jy dit vandag in jou lewe toepas?"]},{"day":2,"title_en":"Genesis 5–8","title_af":"Genesis 5–8","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":5},{"book_id":1,"chapter":6},{"book_id":1,"chapter":7},{"book_id":1,"chapter":8}],"commentary_intro_en":"You are reading through the whole Bible. Each chapter summary below helps you grasp the main story before you read the verses.","commentary_intro_af":"Jy lees deur die hele Bybel. Elke hoofstuk-opsomming hieronder help jou die hoofverhaal te begryp voordat jy die verse lees.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":5,"heading_en":"Genesis 5","heading_af":"Genesis 5","summary_en":"The godly line from Seth to Noah; long lifespans. Wickedness fills the earth. Enoch walks with God and is taken. Noah is born with hope that comfort will come from the curse. Read Genesis 5 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"Die godvrugtige lyn van Seth tot Noag; lank lewensduur. Wickedheid vul die aarde. Enog loop met God en word weggeneem. Noag word gebore met hoop dat troos uit die vloek sal kom. Lees Genesis 5 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Genealogy links Adam to Noah","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 5 records or illustrates: Genealogy links Adam to Noah. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Enoch: extraordinary faith — walks with God","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 5 records or illustrates: Enoch: extraordinary faith — walks with God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Human evil grieves God''s heart","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 5 records or illustrates: Human evil grieves God''s heart. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Noah named for future relief","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 5 records or illustrates: Noah named for future relief. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Genealogie verbind Adam met Noag","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 5 noem of illustreer: Genealogie verbind Adam met Noag. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Enog: buitengewone geloof — loop met God","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 5 noem of illustreer: Enog: buitengewone geloof — loop met God. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Menslike boosheid bedroef God se hart","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 5 noem of illustreer: Menslike boosheid bedroef God se hart. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Noag genoem vir toekomstige verligting","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 5 noem of illustreer: Noag genoem vir toekomstige verligting. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."}],"teaching_en":"God wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.","teaching_why_en":"Pentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.","teaching_af":"God wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.","teaching_why_af":"Pentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"Genesis 4","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 4, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 30:15–20","comparison":"Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Genesis 4","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 4, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander."},{"ref":"Deuteronomium 30:15–20","comparison":"Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel."}],"highlights_en":["Genealogy links Adam to Noah","Enoch: extraordinary faith — walks with God","Human evil grieves God''s heart","Noah named for future relief"],"highlights_af":["Genealogie verbind Adam met Noag","Enog: buitengewone geloof — loop met God","Menslike boosheid bedroef God se hart","Noag genoem vir toekomstige verligting"]},{"book_id":1,"chapter":6,"heading_en":"Genesis 6","heading_af":"Genesis 6","summary_en":"God commands Noah to build an ark; Noah obeys. Pairs of animals enter; the flood destroys the earth. Only Noah''s family and the ark survive — judgment and grace together. Read Genesis 6 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"God beveel Noag om ''n ark te bou; Noag gehoorsaam. Dierepare gaan in; die vloed vernietig die aarde. Net Noag se gesin en die ark oorleef — vonnis en genade saam. Lees Genesis 6 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Total corruption warrants judgment","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 6 records or illustrates: Total corruption warrants judgment. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Noah finds favor through faith and obedience","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 6 records or illustrates: Noah finds favor through faith and obedience. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"The ark: salvation through God''s provision","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 6 records or illustrates: The ark: salvation through God''s provision. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Forty days of rain — a new beginning","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 6 records or illustrates: Forty days of rain — a new beginning. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Totale verdorwenheid regverdig vonnis","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 6 noem of illustreer: Totale verdorwenheid regverdig vonnis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Noag vind genade deur geloof en gehoorsaamheid","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 6 noem of illustreer: Noag vind genade deur geloof en gehoorsaamheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Die ark: redding deur God se voorsiening","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 6 noem of illustreer: Die ark: redding deur God se voorsiening. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Veertig dae reën — ''n nuwe begin","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 6 noem of illustreer: Veertig dae reën — ''n nuwe begin. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."}],"teaching_en":"God wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.","teaching_why_en":"Pentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.","teaching_af":"God wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.","teaching_why_af":"Pentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"1 Peter 3:20","comparison":"Genesis 6–7 records Noah, the ark, and the flood. 1 Peter 3:20 refers to Noah and the saving of eight persons through water — the same flood narrative."},{"ref":"Matthew 24:37–39","comparison":"Jesus compares days before his coming to the days before the flood — eating, drinking, marrying — until the flood came (Genesis 7). Matthew records Jesus citing that era as people living unaware until judgment."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"1 Petrus 3:20","comparison":"Genesis 6–7 beskryf Noag, die ark en die vloed. 1 Petrus 3:20 verwys na Noag en die redding van agt persone deur water."},{"ref":"Matteus 24:37–39","comparison":"Jesus vergelyk dae voor sy koms met dae voor die vloed — eet, drink, trou — tot die vloed gekom het (Gen 7). Matteus beskryf Jesus wat daardie tyd aanhaal."}],"highlights_en":["Total corruption warrants judgment","Noah finds favor through faith and obedience","The ark: salvation through God''s provision","Forty days of rain — a new beginning"],"highlights_af":["Totale verdorwenheid regverdig vonnis","Noag vind genade deur geloof en gehoorsaamheid","Die ark: redding deur God se voorsiening","Veertig dae reën — ''n nuwe begin"]},{"book_id":1,"chapter":7,"heading_en":"Genesis 7","heading_af":"Genesis 7","summary_en":"Waters recede; Noah exits the ark and worships. God establishes the Noahic covenant — never again a flood. The rainbow signs His promise; Noah plants a vineyard. Read Genesis 7 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"Waters sak; Noag gaan uit die ark en aanbid. God stel die Noagiese verbond — nooit weer ''n vloed. Die reënboog teken Sy belofte; Noag plant ''n wingerd. Lees Genesis 7 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Burnt offering — worship after deliverance","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Burnt offering — worship after deliverance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Covenant with all creation","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Covenant with all creation. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Rainbow as sign of mercy","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Rainbow as sign of mercy. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Human responsibility continues after judgment","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Human responsibility continues after judgment. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Verbond met die hele skepping","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Verbond met die hele skepping. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."}],"teaching_en":"God wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.","teaching_why_en":"Pentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.","teaching_af":"God wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.","teaching_why_af":"Pentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"Genesis 6","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 6, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 30:15–20","comparison":"Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Genesis 6","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 6, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander."},{"ref":"Deuteronomium 30:15–20","comparison":"Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel."}],"highlights_en":["Burnt offering — worship after deliverance","Covenant with all creation","Rainbow as sign of mercy","Human responsibility continues after judgment"],"highlights_af":["Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding","Verbond met die hele skepping","Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid","Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis"]},{"book_id":1,"chapter":8,"heading_en":"Genesis 8","heading_af":"Genesis 8","summary_en":"Noah''s sin and Ham''s dishonor; Noah prophesies over his sons. Nations descend from Shem, Ham, and Japheth — the table of peoples begins. Read Genesis 8 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"Noag se sonde en Ham se oneer; Noag profeteer oor sy seuns. Nasies spruit uit Sem, Ham en Jafet — die tabel van volke begin. Lees Genesis 8 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Even the saved remain fallen","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Even the saved remain fallen. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Honor and dishonor within families","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Honor and dishonor within families. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Shem''s line leads toward Abraham","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Shem''s line leads toward Abraham. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Nations spread across the earth","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Nations spread across the earth. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Selfs die geredes bly gevallenes","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Selfs die geredes bly gevallenes. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Eer en oneer binne gesinne","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Eer en oneer binne gesinne. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Sem se lyn lei na Abraham","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Sem se lyn lei na Abraham. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Nasies versprei oor die aarde","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Nasies versprei oor die aarde. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."}],"teaching_en":"God wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.","teaching_why_en":"Pentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.","teaching_af":"God wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.","teaching_why_af":"Pentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"Genesis 7","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 7, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 30:15–20","comparison":"Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Genesis 7","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 7, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander."},{"ref":"Deuteronomium 30:15–20","comparison":"Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel."}],"highlights_en":["Even the saved remain fallen","Honor and dishonor within families","Shem''s line leads toward Abraham","Nations spread across the earth"],"highlights_af":["Selfs die geredes bly gevallenes","Eer en oneer binne gesinne","Sem se lyn lei na Abraham","Nasies versprei oor die aarde"]}],"commentary_en":"You are reading through the whole Bible. Each chapter summary below helps you grasp the main story before you read the verses.\n\nGenesis 5\nThe godly line from Seth to Noah; long lifespans. Wickedness fills the earth. Enoch walks with God and is taken. Noah is born with hope that comfort will come from the curse. Read Genesis 5 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nGenealogy links Adam to Noah\nThe text of Genesis 5 records or illustrates: Genealogy links Adam to Noah. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nEnoch: extraordinary faith — walks with God\nThe text of Genesis 5 records or illustrates: Enoch: extraordinary faith — walks with God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHuman evil grieves God''s heart\nThe text of Genesis 5 records or illustrates: Human evil grieves God''s heart. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNoah named for future relief\nThe text of Genesis 5 records or illustrates: Noah named for future relief. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 4: Compare with Genesis 4, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 6\nGod commands Noah to build an ark; Noah obeys. Pairs of animals enter; the flood destroys the earth. Only Noah''s family and the ark survive — judgment and grace together. Read Genesis 6 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nTotal corruption warrants judgment\nThe text of Genesis 6 records or illustrates: Total corruption warrants judgment. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNoah finds favor through faith and obedience\nThe text of Genesis 6 records or illustrates: Noah finds favor through faith and obedience. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nThe ark: salvation through God''s provision\nThe text of Genesis 6 records or illustrates: The ark: salvation through God''s provision. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nForty days of rain — a new beginning\nThe text of Genesis 6 records or illustrates: Forty days of rain — a new beginning. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\n1 Peter 3:20: Genesis 6–7 records Noah, the ark, and the flood. 1 Peter 3:20 refers to Noah and the saving of eight persons through water — the same flood narrative.\nMatthew 24:37–39: Jesus compares days before his coming to the days before the flood — eating, drinking, marrying — until the flood came (Genesis 7). Matthew records Jesus citing that era as people living unaware until judgment.\n\nGenesis 7\nWaters recede; Noah exits the ark and worships. God establishes the Noahic covenant — never again a flood. The rainbow signs His promise; Noah plants a vineyard. Read Genesis 7 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nBurnt offering — worship after deliverance\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Burnt offering — worship after deliverance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nCovenant with all creation\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Covenant with all creation. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nRainbow as sign of mercy\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Rainbow as sign of mercy. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHuman responsibility continues after judgment\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Human responsibility continues after judgment. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 6: Compare with Genesis 6, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 8\nNoah''s sin and Ham''s dishonor; Noah prophesies over his sons. Nations descend from Shem, Ham, and Japheth — the table of peoples begins. Read Genesis 8 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nEven the saved remain fallen\nThe text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Even the saved remain fallen. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHonor and dishonor within families\nThe text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Honor and dishonor within families. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nShem''s line leads toward Abraham\nThe text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Shem''s line leads toward Abraham. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNations spread across the earth\nThe text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Nations spread across the earth. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 7: Compare with Genesis 7, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.","commentary_af":"Jy lees deur die hele Bybel. Elke hoofstuk-opsomming hieronder help jou die hoofverhaal te begryp voordat jy die verse lees.\n\nGenesis 5\nDie godvrugtige lyn van Seth tot Noag; lank lewensduur. Wickedheid vul die aarde. Enog loop met God en word weggeneem. Noag word gebore met hoop dat troos uit die vloek sal kom. Lees Genesis 5 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nGenealogie verbind Adam met Noag\nDie teks van Genesis 5 noem of illustreer: Genealogie verbind Adam met Noag. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nEnog: buitengewone geloof — loop met God\nDie teks van Genesis 5 noem of illustreer: Enog: buitengewone geloof — loop met God. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nMenslike boosheid bedroef God se hart\nDie teks van Genesis 5 noem of illustreer: Menslike boosheid bedroef God se hart. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nNoag genoem vir toekomstige verligting\nDie teks van Genesis 5 noem of illustreer: Noag genoem vir toekomstige verligting. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 4: Compare with Genesis 4, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 6\nGod beveel Noag om ''n ark te bou; Noag gehoorsaam. Dierepare gaan in; die vloed vernietig die aarde. Net Noag se gesin en die ark oorleef — vonnis en genade saam. Lees Genesis 6 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nTotale verdorwenheid regverdig vonnis\nDie teks van Genesis 6 noem of illustreer: Totale verdorwenheid regverdig vonnis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nNoag vind genade deur geloof en gehoorsaamheid\nDie teks van Genesis 6 noem of illustreer: Noag vind genade deur geloof en gehoorsaamheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nDie ark: redding deur God se voorsiening\nDie teks van Genesis 6 noem of illustreer: Die ark: redding deur God se voorsiening. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nVeertig dae reën — ''n nuwe begin\nDie teks van Genesis 6 noem of illustreer: Veertig dae reën — ''n nuwe begin. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\n1 Petrus 3:20: Genesis 6–7 beskryf Noag, die ark en die vloed. 1 Petrus 3:20 verwys na Noag en die redding van agt persone deur water.\nMatteus 24:37–39: Jesus vergelyk dae voor sy koms met dae voor die vloed — eet, drink, trou — tot die vloed gekom het (Gen 7). Matteus beskryf Jesus wat daardie tyd aanhaal.\n\nGenesis 7\nWaters sak; Noag gaan uit die ark en aanbid. God stel die Noagiese verbond — nooit weer ''n vloed. Die reënboog teken Sy belofte; Noag plant ''n wingerd. Lees Genesis 7 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nBrandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nVerbond met die hele skepping\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Verbond met die hele skepping. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nReënboog as teken van barmhartigheid\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nMenslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 6: Compare with Genesis 6, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 8\nNoag se sonde en Ham se oneer; Noag profeteer oor sy seuns. Nasies spruit uit Sem, Ham en Jafet — die tabel van volke begin. Lees Genesis 8 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nSelfs die geredes bly gevallenes\nDie teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Selfs die geredes bly gevallenes. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nEer en oneer binne gesinne\nDie teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Eer en oneer binne gesinne. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nSem se lyn lei na Abraham\nDie teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Sem se lyn lei na Abraham. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nNasies versprei oor die aarde\nDie teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Nasies versprei oor die aarde. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 7: Compare with Genesis 7, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.","questions_en":["What does this passage reveal about God?","What promise, command, or truth stands out to you?","How can you apply this to your life today?"],"questions_af":["Wat openbaar hierdie gedeelte oor God?","Watter belofte, gebod of waarheid val vir jou op?","Hoe kan jy dit vandag in jou lewe toepas?"]},{"day":3,"title_en":"Genesis 9–12","title_af":"Genesis 9–12","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":9},{"book_id":1,"chapter":10},{"book_id":1,"chapter":11},{"book_id":1,"chapter":12}],"commentary_intro_en":"You are reading through the whole Bible. Each chapter summary below helps you grasp the main story before you read the verses.","commentary_intro_af":"Jy lees deur die hele Bybel. Elke hoofstuk-opsomming hieronder help jou die hoofverhaal te begryp voordat jy die verse lees.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":9,"heading_en":"Genesis 9","heading_af":"Genesis 9","summary_en":"Humanity unites to build a tower to make a name for themselves. God confuses their language and scatters them. Babel explains diverse languages; pride is judged. Read Genesis 9 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"Die mensheid verenig om ''n toring te bou vir eie roem. God verwar hul taal en versprei hulle. Babel verduidelik diverse tale; hoogmoed word beoordeel. Lees Genesis 9 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Unity apart from God leads to rebellion","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: Unity apart from God leads to rebellion. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Self-glory replaces God''s glory","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: Self-glory replaces God''s glory. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Languages divided — scattering","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: Languages divided — scattering. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"From Babel to Abram: God''s plan continues","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: From Babel to Abram: God''s plan continues. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Eenheid sonder God lei tot rebelle","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 9 noem of illustreer: Eenheid sonder God lei tot rebelle. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Self-roem vervang God se heerlikheid","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 9 noem of illustreer: Self-roem vervang God se heerlikheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Tale verdeel — verspreiding","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 9 noem of illustreer: Tale verdeel — verspreiding. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Van Babel na Abram: God se plan gaan voort","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 9 noem of illustreer: Van Babel na Abram: God se plan gaan voort. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."}],"teaching_en":"God wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.","teaching_why_en":"Pentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.","teaching_af":"God wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.","teaching_why_af":"Pentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"Genesis 8","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 8, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 30:15–20","comparison":"Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Genesis 8","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 8, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander."},{"ref":"Deuteronomium 30:15–20","comparison":"Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel."}],"highlights_en":["Unity apart from God leads to rebellion","Self-glory replaces God''s glory","Languages divided — scattering","From Babel to Abram: God''s plan continues"],"highlights_af":["Eenheid sonder God lei tot rebelle","Self-roem vervang God se heerlikheid","Tale verdeel — verspreiding","Van Babel na Abram: God se plan gaan voort"]},{"book_id":1,"chapter":10,"heading_en":"Genesis 10","heading_af":"Genesis 10","summary_en":"Genealogy from Shem to Abram. Terah moves toward Canaan; Abram is called. The stage is set for God''s covenant with Abraham and the promise to bless all nations. Read Genesis 10 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"Genealogie van Sem tot Abram. Terah beweeg na Kanaän; Abram word geroep. Die verhoog word gereed vir God se verbond met Abraham en die belofte om alle nasies te seën. Lees Genesis 10 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Shem''s line preserved through generations","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 10 records or illustrates: Shem''s line preserved through generations. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Ur to Haran to Canaan — a journey of faith","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 10 records or illustrates: Ur to Haran to Canaan — a journey of faith. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Abram appears at the end of the list","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 10 records or illustrates: Abram appears at the end of the list. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Transition from primeval history to patriarchs","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 10 records or illustrates: Transition from primeval history to patriarchs. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Sem se lyn bewaar deur geslagte","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 10 noem of illustreer: Sem se lyn bewaar deur geslagte. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Ur na Haran na Kanaän — ''n reis van geloof","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 10 noem of illustreer: Ur na Haran na Kanaän — ''n reis van geloof. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Abram verskyn aan die einde van die lys","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 10 noem of illustreer: Abram verskyn aan die einde van die lys. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Oorgang van vroeg