themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 22\nAbraham tested — offers Isaac; angel stops him; ram provided; covenant reaffirmed. Read Genesis 22 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nKey events in God''s covenant story\nThe text of Genesis 22 records or illustrates: Key events in God''s covenant story. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWatch how promises survive human failure\nThe text of Genesis 22 records or illustrates: Watch how promises survive human failure. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNote worship, obedience, and grace\nThe text of Genesis 22 records or illustrates: Note worship, obedience, and grace. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 21: Compare with Genesis 21, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 23\nSarah dies; Abraham buys Machpelah cave — first foothold in the promised land. Read Genesis 23 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nKey events in God''s covenant story\nThe text of Genesis 23 records or illustrates: Key events in God''s covenant story. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWatch how promises survive human failure\nThe text of Genesis 23 records or illustrates: Watch how promises survive human failure. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNote worship, obedience, and grace\nThe text of Genesis 23 records or illustrates: Note worship, obedience, and grace. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 22: Compare with Genesis 22, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 24\nServant sent to find Isaac a wife; Rebekah chosen; faithful prayer answered. Read Genesis 24 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nKey events in God''s covenant story\nThe text of Genesis 24 records or illustrates: Key events in God''s covenant story. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWatch how promises survive human failure\nThe text of Genesis 24 records or illustrates: Watch how promises survive human failure. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNote worship, obedience, and grace\nThe text of Genesis 24 records or illustrates: Note worship, obedience, and grace. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 23: Compare with Genesis 23, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.","commentary_af":"Jy lees deur die hele Bybel. Elke hoofstuk-opsomming hieronder help jou die hoofverhaal te begryp voordat jy die verse lees.\n\nGenesis 21\nIsak gebore; Hagar en Ismael weggestuur; verdrag by Bersheba. Lees Genesis 21 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nSleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal\nDie teks van Genesis 21 noem of illustreer: Sleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef\nDie teks van Genesis 21 noem of illustreer: Let hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade\nDie teks van Genesis 21 noem of illustreer: Let op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 20: Compare with Genesis 20, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 22\nAbraham getoets — offer Isak; engel keer hom; ram voorsien; verbond bekragtig. Lees Genesis 22 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nSleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal\nDie teks van Genesis 22 noem of illustreer: Sleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef\nDie teks van Genesis 22 noem of illustreer: Let hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade\nDie teks van Genesis 22 noem of illustreer: Let op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 21: Compare with Genesis 21, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 23\nSara sterf; Abraham koop die Makpela-grot — eerste voet aan die grond in die beloofde land. Lees Genesis 23 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nSleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal\nDie teks van Genesis 23 noem of illustreer: Sleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef\nDie teks van Genesis 23 noem of illustreer: Let hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade\nDie teks van Genesis 23 noem of illustreer: Let op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 22: Compare with Genesis 22, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 24\nDienskneg gestuur om vrou vir Isak te vind; Rebekka gekies; getroue gebed beantwoord. Lees Genesis 24 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nSleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal\nDie teks van Genesis 24 noem of illustreer: Sleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef\nDie teks van Genesis 24 noem of illustreer: Let hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade\nDie teks van Genesis 24 noem of illustreer: Let op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 23: Compare with Genesis 23, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.","questions_en":["What does this passage reveal about God?","What promise, command, or truth stands out to you?","How can you apply this to your life today?"],"questions_af":["Wat openbaar hierdie gedeelte oor God?","Watter belofte, gebod of waarheid val vir jou op?","Hoe kan jy dit vandag in jou lewe toepas?"]},{"day":7,"title_en":"Genesis 25–28","title_af":"Genesis 25–28","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":25},{"book_id":1,"chapter":26},{"book_id":1,"chapter":27},{"book_id":1,"chapter":28}],"commentary_intro_en":"You are reading through the whole Bible. Each chapter summary below helps you grasp the main story before you read the verses.","commentary_intro_af":"Jy lees deur die hele Bybel. Elke hoofstuk-opsomming hieronder help jou die hoofverhaal te begryp voordat jy die verse lees.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":25,"heading_en":"Genesis 25","heading_af":"Genesis 25","summary_en":"Abraham dies; Esau and Jacob born; birthright sold for stew. Read Genesis 25 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"Abraham sterf; Esau en Jakob gebore; geboorte reg verkoop vir bredie. Lees Genesis 25 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Key events 