 death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 34\nDinah violated; Simeon and Levi''s violent revenge. Read Genesis 34 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nKey events in God''s covenant story\nThe text of Genesis 34 records or illustrates: Key events in God''s covenant story. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWatch how promises survive human failure\nThe text of Genesis 34 records or illustrates: Watch how promises survive human failure. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNote worship, obedience, and grace\nThe text of Genesis 34 records or illustrates: Note worship, obedience, and grace. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 33: Compare with Genesis 33, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 35\nGod tells Jacob to go to Bethel; Rachel dies; Isaac dies. Read Genesis 35 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nKey events in God''s covenant story\nThe text of Genesis 35 records or illustrates: Key events in God''s covenant story. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWatch how promises survive human failure\nThe text of Ge