rd what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nExodus 36: Compare with Exodus 36, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nExodus 38\nExodus 38. This chapter advances the story of God''s covenant people — watch for promises, obedience, and how God keeps His word despite human failure. Read Exodus 38 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nCovenant promises\nThe text of Exodus 38 records or illustrates: Covenant promises. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nObedience and consequences\nThe text of Exodus 38 records or illustrates: Obedience and consequences. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s faithfulness\nThe text of Exodus 38 records or illustrates: God''s faithfulness. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nExodus 37: Compare with Exodus 37, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.","commentary_af":"Jy lees deur die hele Bybel. Elke hoofstuk-opsomming hieronder help jou die hoofverhaal te begryp voordat jy die verse lees.\n\nEksodus 35\nEksodus 35. Hierdie hoofstuk beweeg die verhaal van God se verbondsvolk vorentoe — let op beloftes, gehoorsaamheid en hoe God Sy woord hou ondanks menslike mislukking. Lees Eksodus 35 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nVerbondbeloftes\nDie teks van Eksodus 35 noem of illustreer: Verbondbeloftes. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGehoorsaamheid en gevolge\nDie teks van Eksodus 35 noem of illustreer: Gehoorsaamheid en gevolge. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGod se getrouheid\nDie teks van Eksodus 35 noem of illustreer: God se getrouheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nEksodus 34: Compare with Eksodus 34, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nEksodus 36\nEksodus 36. Hierdie hoofstuk beweeg die verhaal van God se verbondsvolk vorentoe — let op beloftes, gehoorsaamheid en hoe God Sy woord hou ondanks menslike mislukking. Lees Eksodus 36 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nVerbondbeloftes\nDie teks van Eksodus 36 noem of illustreer: Verbondbeloftes. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê