pplies that foundation differently.\n\nProverbs 26\nAnswer not a fool; lazy sluggard; quarrelsome wife. Read Proverbs 26 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nPractical wisdom for daily choices\nThe text of Proverbs 26 records or illustrates: Practical wisdom for daily choices. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFear of the Lord as foundation\nThe text of Proverbs 26 records or illustrates: Fear of the Lord as foundation. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nOne principle to practice today\nThe text of Proverbs 26 records or illustrates: One principle to practice today. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 20 25: Compare with Book 20 25, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1:7: Wisdom books often repeat the theme that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Proverbs 1:7). Compare how each psalm or proverb applies that foundation differently.\n\nProverbs 27\nDo not boast in tomorrow; open rebuke; iron sharpens iron. Read Proverbs 27 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nPractical wisdom for daily choices\nThe text of Proverbs 27 records or illustrates: Practical wisdom for daily choices. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFear of the Lord as foundation\nThe text of Proverbs 27 records or illustrates: Fear of the Lord as foundation. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nOne principle to practice today\nThe text o