 your Bible version.\n\nJesus'' identity and authority\nThe text of Luke 7 records or illustrates: Jesus'' identity and authority. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nKingdom teaching\nThe text of Luke 7 records or illustrates: Kingdom teaching. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nCalls to follow\nThe text of Luke 7 records or illustrates: Calls to follow. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know who Jesus is and what he calls people to believe and do through his words, actions, and authority.\n\nWhy:\nGospel chapters record what Jesus taught, healed, commanded, or revealed about the kingdom. Focus on what the chapter says Jesus did and said—not later tradition.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 42 6: Compare with Book 42 6, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nParallel Gospels: Many events in Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John appear in more than one Gospel with matching or overlapping details. Compare the same episode across Gospels to see what each writer records.\n\nLuke 8\nLuke 8. The Gospels reveal Jesus — His teaching, authority, compassion, and mission. Ask: Who is Jesus here, and what is He calling me to believe and do? Read Luke 8 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nJesus'' identity and authority\nThe text of Luke 8 records or illustrates: Jesus'' identity and authority. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nKingdom teaching\nThe text of Luke 8 records or illustrates: Kingdom teaching. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nCalls to follow\nThe text of Luke 8 records or illustrates: Calls to follow. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know who Jesus is and what he calls people to believe and do through his 