 text of Revelation 14 records or illustrates: Judgment and mercy. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNew creation hope\nThe text of Revelation 14 records or illustrates: New creation hope. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that Christ reigns, justice will be done, and God will renew creation for his people.\n\nWhy:\nRevelation uses visions of throne, Lamb, judgment, and new creation. Identify what this chapter records about Christ''s authority and the future God promises.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 66 13: Compare with Book 66 13, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\n\nRevelation 15\nRevelation 15. Apocalyptic vision of Christ''s victory and final renewal. Balance symbolic imagery with the certainty that God wins and His people inherit eternal life. Read Revelation 15 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nChrist the victorious Lamb\nThe text of Revelation 15 records or illustrates: Christ the victorious Lamb. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nJudgment and mercy\nThe text of Revelation 15 records or illustrates: Judgment and mercy. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNew creation hope\nThe text of Revelation 15 records or illustrates: New creation hope. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that Christ reigns, justice will be done, and God will renew creation for his people.\n\nWhy:\nRevelation uses visions of throne, Lamb, judgment, and new creation. Identify what this chapter records about Christ''s authority and the future God promises.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 66 14: Compare with Book 66 14, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\n\nRevelation