die aarde deur Abram te seën. Gal 3:8 verwys na Skrif wat voorsien dat God die nasies deur geloof regverdig."}],"highlights_en":["Key events in God''s covenant story","Watch how promises survive human failure","Note worship, obedience, and grace"],"highlights_af":["Sleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal","Let hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef","Let op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade"]}],"commentary_en":"Old Testament narrative and teaching. Use the chapter summaries to track God''s covenant story across history.\n\nGenesis 7\nWaters recede; Noah exits the ark and worships. God establishes the Noahic covenant — never again a flood. The rainbow signs His promise; Noah plants a vineyard. Read Genesis 7 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nBurnt offering — worship after deliverance\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Burnt offering — worship after deliverance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nCovenant with all creation\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Covenant with all creation. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nRainbow as sign of mercy\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Rainbow as sign of mercy. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHuman responsibility continues after judgment\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Human responsibility continues after judgment. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 6: Compare with Genesis 6, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 8\nNoah''s sin and Ham''s dishonor; Noah prophesies over his sons. Nations descend from Shem, Ham, and Japheth — the table of peoples begins. Read Genesis 8 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nEven the saved remain fallen\nThe text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Even the saved remain fallen. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHonor and dishonor within families\nThe text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Honor and dishonor within families. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nShem''s line leads toward Abraham\nThe text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Shem''s line leads toward Abraham. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNations spread across the earth\nThe text of Genesis 8 records or illustrates: Nations spread across the earth. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 7: Compare with Genesis 7, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 9\nHumanity unites to build a tower to make a name for themselves. God confuses their language and scatters them. Babel explains diverse languages; pride is judged. Read Genesis 9 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nUnity apart from God leads to rebellion\nThe text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: Unity apart from God leads to rebellion. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nSelf-glory replaces God''s glory\nThe text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: Self-glory replaces God''s glory. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nLanguages divided — scattering\nThe text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: Languages divided — scattering. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFrom Babel to Abram: God''s plan continues\nThe text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: From Babel to Abram: God''s plan continues. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 8: Compare with Genesis 8, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 10\nGenealogy from Shem to Abram. Terah moves toward Canaan; Abram is called. The stage is set for God''s covenant with Abraham and the promise to bless all nations. Read Genesis 10 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nShem''s line preserved through generations\nThe text of Genesis 10 records or illustrates: Shem''s line preserved through generations. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nUr to Haran to Canaan — a journey of faith\nThe text of Genesis 10 records or illustrates: Ur to Haran to Canaan — a journey of faith. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nAbram appears at the end of the list\nThe text of Genesis 10 records or illustrates: Abram appears at the end of the list. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nTransition from primeval history to patriarchs\nThe text of Genesis 10 records or illustrates: Transition from primeval history to patriarchs. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 9: Compare with Genesis 9, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 11\nGod calls Abram to leave his country; Abram obeys. In Canaan God promises the land to his offspring. Abram builds altars; famine drives him to Egypt where he fails but God protects. Read Genesis 11 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nThe call: go to a land I will show you\nThe text of Genesis 11 records or illustrates: The call: go to a land I will show you. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nPromise of blessing and a great nation\nThe text of Genesis 11 records or illustrates: Promise of blessing and a great nation. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nAltars mark places of worship\nThe text of Genesis 11 records or illustrates: Altars mark places of worship. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFear in Egypt — yet God preserves the promise\nThe text of Genesis 11 records or illustrates: Fear in Egypt — yet God preserves the promise. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 10: Compare with Genesis 10, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nGenesis 12\nAbram and Lot separate; Abram rescues Lot; Melchizedek blesses; God''s covenant confirmed. Read Genesis 12 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nKey events in God''s covenant story\nThe text of Genesis 12 records or illustrates: Key events in God''s covenant story. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWatch how promises survive human failure\nThe text of Genesis 12 records or illustrates: Watch how promises survive human failure. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNote worship, obedience, and grace\nThe text of Genesis 12 records or illustrates: Note worship, obedience, and grace. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nActs 7:2–4: Genesis 12 records God calling Abram to leave his country. Stephen in Acts 7:2–4 recounts the God of glory appearing to Abraham in Mesopotamia and telling him to leave — the same call narrative.\nGalatians 3:8: Genesis 12:3 records God promising to bless all families of the earth through Abram. Galatians 3:8 cites Scripture foreseeing that God would justify the nations by faith — quoting that promise theme.","commentary_af":"Ou Testament verhaal en lering. Gebruik die hoofstuk-opsommings om God se verbondverhaal deur die geskiedenis te volg.\n\nGenesis 7\nWaters sak; Noag gaan uit die ark en aanbid. God stel die Noagiese verbond — nooit weer ''n vloed. Die reënboog teken Sy belofte; Noag plant ''n wingerd. Lees Genesis 7 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nBrandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nVerbond met die hele skepping\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Verbond met die hele skepping. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nReënboog as teken van barmhartigheid\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nMenslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 6: Compare with Genesis 6, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 8\nNoag se sonde en Ham se oneer; Noag profeteer oor sy seuns. Nasies spruit uit Sem, Ham en Jafet — die tabel van volke begin. Lees Genesis 8 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nSelfs die geredes bly gevallenes\nDie teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Selfs die geredes bly gevallenes. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nEer en oneer binne gesinne\nDie teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Eer en oneer binne gesinne. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nSem se lyn lei na Abraham\nDie teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Sem se lyn lei na Abraham. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nNasies versprei oor die aarde\nDie teks van Genesis 8 noem of illustreer: Nasies versprei oor die aarde. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 7: Compare with Genesis 7, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 9\nDie mensheid verenig om ''n toring te bou vir eie roem. God verwar hul taal en versprei hulle. Babel verduidelik diverse tale; hoogmoed word beoordeel. Lees Genesis 9 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nEenheid sonder God lei tot rebelle\nDie teks van Genesis 9 noem of illustreer: Eenheid sonder God lei tot rebelle. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nSelf-roem vervang God se heerlikheid\nDie teks van Genesis 9 noem of illustreer: Self-roem vervang God se heerlikheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nTale verdeel — verspreiding\nDie teks van Genesis 9 noem of illustreer: Tale verdeel — verspreiding. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nVan Babel na Abram: God se plan gaan voort\nDie teks van Genesis 9 noem of illustreer: Van Babel na Abram: God se plan gaan voort. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 8: Compare with Genesis 8, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 10\nGenealogie van Sem tot Abram. Terah beweeg na Kanaän; Abram word geroep. Die verhoog word gereed vir God se verbond met Abraham en die belofte om alle nasies te seën. Lees Genesis 10 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nSem se lyn bewaar deur geslagte\nDie teks van Genesis 10 noem of illustreer: Sem se lyn bewaar deur geslagte. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nUr na Haran na Kanaän — ''n reis van geloof\nDie teks van Genesis 10 noem of illustreer: Ur na Haran na Kanaän — ''n reis van geloof. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nAbram verskyn aan die einde van die lys\nDie teks van Genesis 10 noem of illustreer: Abram verskyn aan die einde van die lys. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nOorgang van vroegste geskiedenis na patriarge\nDie teks van Genesis 10 noem of illustreer: Oorgang van vroegste geskiedenis na patriarge. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 9: Compare with Genesis 9, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 11\nGod roep Abram om sy land te verlaat; Abram gehoorsaam. In Kanaän beloof God die land aan sy nageslag. Abram bou altare; hongersnood dryf hom na Egipte waar hy faal maar God beskerm. Lees Genesis 11 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nDie roeping: ga na ''n land wat Ek sal wys\nDie teks van Genesis 11 noem of illustreer: Die roeping: ga na ''n land wat Ek sal wys. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nBelofte van seën en ''n groot nasie\nDie teks van Genesis 11 noem of illustreer: Belofte van seën en ''n groot nasie. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nAltare merk plekke van aanbidding\nDie teks van Genesis 11 noem of illustreer: Altare merk plekke van aanbidding. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nVrees in Egipte — tog bewaar God die belofte\nDie teks van Genesis 11 noem of illustreer: Vrees in Egipte — tog bewaar God die belofte. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 10: Compare with Genesis 10, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nGenesis 12\nAbram en Lot skei; Abram red Lot; Melchisedek seën; God se verbond bevestig. Lees Genesis 12 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nSleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal\nDie teks van Genesis 12 noem of illustreer: Sleutelgebeure in God se verbondverhaal. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef\nDie teks van Genesis 12 noem of illustreer: Let hoe beloftes menslike mislukking oorleef. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nLet op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade\nDie teks van Genesis 12 noem of illustreer: Let op aanbidding, gehoorsaamheid en genade. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nHand 7:2–4: Genesis 12 beskryf God wat Abram roep om sy land te verlaat. Stefanus in Hand 7:2–4 herhaal dat die God van heerlikheid aan Abraham verskyn het en hom gesê het om te trek.\nGalasiërs 3:8: Genesis 12:3 beskryf God se belofte om alle geslagte van die aarde deur Abram te seën. Gal 3:8 verwys na Skrif wat voorsien dat God die nasies deur geloof regverdig.","questions_en":["What does this passage reveal about God?","What promise, command, or truth stands out to you?","How can you apply this to your life today?"],"questions_af":["Wat openbaar hierdie gedeelte oor God?","Watter belofte, gebod of waarheid val vir jou op?","Hoe kan jy dit vandag in jou lewe toepas?"]},{"day":3,"title_en":"Genesis 13–18","title_af":"Genesis 13–18","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":13},{"book_id":1,"chapter":14},{"book_id":1,"chapter":15},{"book_id":1,"chapter":16},{"book_id":1,"chapter":17},{"book_id":1,"chapter":18}],"commentary_intro_en":"Old Testament narrative and teaching. Use the chapter summaries to track God''s covenant story across history.","commentary_intro_af":"Ou Testament verhaal en lering. Gebruik die hoofstuk-opsommings om God se verbondverhaal deur die geskiedenis te volg.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":13,"heading_en":"Genesis 13","heading_af":"Genesis 