ge","God se getrouheid"]}],"commentary_en":"Old Testament narrative and teaching. Use the chapter summaries to track God''s covenant story across history.\n\nDeuteronomy 4\nDeuteronomy 4. This chapter advances the story of God''s covenant people — watch for promises, obedience, and how God keeps His word despite human failure. Read Deuteronomy 4 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nCovenant promises\nThe text of Deuteronomy 4 records or illustrates: Covenant promises. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nObedience and consequences\nThe text of Deuteronomy 4 records or illustrates: Obedience and consequences. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s faithfulness\nThe text of Deuteronomy 4 records or illustrates: God''s faithfulness. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 5 3: Compare with Book 5 3, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nDeuteronomy 5\nDeuteronomy 5. This chapter advances the story of God''s covenant people — watch for promises, obedience, and how God keeps His word despite human failure. Read Deuteronomy 5 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nCovenant promises\nThe text of Deuteronomy 5 records or i