ersion.\n\nCovenant promises\nThe text of Deuteronomy 32 records or illustrates: Covenant promises. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nObedience and consequences\nThe text of Deuteronomy 32 records or illustrates: Obedience and consequences. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s faithfulness\nThe text of Deuteronomy 32 records or illustrates: God''s faithfulness. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 5 31: Compare with Book 5 31, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nDeuteronomy 33\nDeuteronomy 33. This chapter advances the story of God''s covenant people — watch for promises, obedience, and how God keeps His word despite human failure. Read Deuteronomy 33 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nCovenant promises\nThe text of Deuteronomy 33 records or illustrates: Covenant promises. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nObedience and consequences\nThe text of Deuteronomy 33 records or illustrates: Obedience and consequences. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s faithfulness\nThe text of Deuteronomy 33 records or illustrates: God''s faithfulness. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 5 32: Compare with Book 5 32, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nDeuteronomy 34\nDeuteronomy 34. This chapter advances the story of God''s covenant people — watch for promises, obedience, and how God keeps His word despite human failure. Read Deuteronomy 34 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nCovenant promises\nThe text of Deuteronomy 34 records or illustrates: Covenant promises. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nObedience and consequences\nThe text of Deuteronomy 34 records or illustrates: Obedience and consequences. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s faithfulness\nThe text of Deuteronomy 34 records or illustrates: God''s faithfulness. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 5 33: Compare with Book 5 33, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.\n\nBook 6 1\nBook 6 1. Historical narrative showing God''s guidance through victory, failure, and restoration. Notice leadership, prayer, and the consequences of turning from God. Read Book 6 1 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nGod''s sovereignty in history\nThe text of Book 6 1 records or illustrates: God''s sovereignty in history. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFaithful vs. unfaithful leaders\nThe text of Book 6 1 records or illustrates: Faithful vs. unfaithful leaders. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nRepentance and restoration\nThe text of Book 6 1 records or illustrates: Repentance and restoration. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to see that he works through history—using leaders, correcting rebellion, and keeping his promises across generations.\n\nWhy:\nHistorical books record outcomes when people follow or abandon God''s command. Notice who leads, what they do, and what the chapter says happened as a result.\n\nBook 6 2\nBook 6 2. Historical narrative showing God''s guidance through victory, failure, and restoration. Notice leadership, prayer, and the consequences of turning from God. Read Book 6 2 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nGod''s sovereignty in history\nThe text of Book 6 2 records or illustrates: God''s sovereignty in history. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFaithful vs. unfaithful leaders\nThe text of Book 6 2 records or illustrates: Faithful vs. unfaithful leaders. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nRepentance and restoration\nThe text of Book 6 2 records or illustrates: Repentance and restoration. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to see that he works through history—using leaders, correcting rebellion, and keeping his promises across generations.\n\nWhy:\nHistorical books record outcomes when people follow or abandon God''s command. Notice who leads, what they do, and what the chapter says happened as a result.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 6 1: Compare with Book 6 1, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.","commentary_af":"Ou Testament verhaal en lering. Gebruik die hoofstuk-opsommings om God se verbondverhaal deur die geskiedenis te volg.\n\nDeuteronomium 32\nDeuteronomium 32. Hierdie hoofstuk beweeg die verhaal van God se verbondsvolk vorentoe — let op beloftes, gehoorsaamheid en hoe God Sy woord hou ondanks menslike mislukking. Lees Deuteronomium 32 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nVerbondbeloftes\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 32 noem of illustreer: Verbondbeloftes. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGehoorsaamheid en gevolge\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 32 noem of illustreer: Gehoorsaamheid en gevolge. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGod se getrouheid\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 32 noem of illustreer: God se getrouheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 5 31: Compare with Book 5 31, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nDeuteronomium 33\nDeuteronomium 33. Hierdie hoofstuk beweeg die verhaal van God se verbondsvolk vorentoe — let op beloftes, gehoorsaamheid en hoe God Sy woord hou ondanks menslike mislukking. Lees Deuteronomium 33 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nVerbondbeloftes\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 33 noem of illustreer: Verbondbeloftes. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGehoorsaamheid en gevolge\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 33 noem of illustreer: Gehoorsaamheid en gevolge. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGod se getrouheid\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 33 noem of illustreer: God se getrouheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 5 32: Compare with Book 5 32, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nDeuteronomium 34\nDeuteronomium 34. Hierdie hoofstuk beweeg die verhaal van God se verbondsvolk vorentoe — let op beloftes, gehoorsaamheid en hoe God Sy woord hou ondanks menslike mislukking. Lees Deuteronomium 34 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nVerbondbeloftes\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 34 noem of illustreer: Verbondbeloftes. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGehoorsaamheid en gevolge\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 34 noem of illustreer: Gehoorsaamheid en gevolge. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGod se getrouheid\nDie teks van Deuteronomium 34 noem of illustreer: God se getrouheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 5 33: Compare with Book 5 33, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.\n\nBoek 6 1\nBoek 6 1. Geskiedenisverhaal wat God se leiding wys deur oorwinning, mislukking en herstel. Let op leierskap, gebed en die gevolge van wegdraai van God. Lees Boek 6 1 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nGod se soewereiniteit in geskiedenis\nDie teks van Boek 6 1 noem of illustreer: God se soewereiniteit in geskiedenis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGetroue vs. ontroue leiers\nDie teks van Boek 6 1 noem of illustreer: Getroue vs. ontroue leiers. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nBerou en herstel\nDie teks van Boek 6 1 noem of illustreer: Berou en herstel. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet sien dat Hy deur geskiedenis werk—leiers gebruik, rebelle regstel, en beloftes oor geslagte heen hou.\n\nWhy:\nGeskiedenisboeke beskryf uitkomste wanneer mense God se gebod volg of verlaat. Let wie lei, wat hulle doen, en wat die hoofstuk sê het gebeur.\n\nBoek 6 2\nBoek 6 2. Geskiedenisverhaal wat God se leiding wys deur oorwinning, mislukking en herstel. Let op leierskap, gebed en die gevolge van wegdraai van God. Lees Boek 6 2 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nGod se soewereiniteit in geskiedenis\nDie teks van Boek 6 2 noem of illustreer: God se soewereiniteit in geskiedenis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nGetroue vs. ontroue leiers\nDie teks van Boek 6 2 noem of illustreer: Getroue vs. ontroue leiers. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nBerou en herstel\nDie teks van Boek 6 2 noem of illustreer: Berou en herstel. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet sien dat Hy deur geskiedenis werk—leiers gebruik, rebelle regstel, en beloftes oor geslagte heen hou.\n\nWhy:\nGeskiedenisboeke beskryf uitkomste wanneer mense God se gebod volg of verlaat. Let wie lei, wat hulle doen, en wat die hoofstuk sê het gebeur.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 6 1: Compare with Book 6 1, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.","questions_en":["What does this passage reveal about God?","What promise, command, or truth stands out to you?","How can you apply this to your life today?"],"questions_af":["Wat openbaar hierdie gedeelte oor God?","Watter belofte, gebod of waarheid val vir jou op?","Hoe kan jy dit vandag in jou lewe toepas?"]},{"day":33,"title_en":"Joshua 3–7","title_af":"Josua 3–7","passages":[{"book_id":6,"chapter":3},{"book_id":6,"chapter":4},{"book_id":6,"chapter":5},{"book_id":6,"chapter":6},{"book_id":6,"chapter":7}],"commentary_intro_en":"Old Testament nar