Faithful vs. unfaithful leaders","Repentance and restoration"],"highlights_af":["God se soewereiniteit in geskiedenis","Getroue vs. ontroue leiers","Berou en herstel"]}],"commentary_en":"Old Testament narrative and teaching. Use the chapter summaries to track God''s covenant story across history.\n\nBook 11 19\nBook 11 19. Historical narrative showing God''s guidance through victory, failure, and restoration. Notice leadership, prayer, and the consequences of turning from God. Read Book 11 19 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nGod''s sovereignty in history\nThe text of Book 11 19 records or illustrates: God''s sovereignty in history. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFaithful vs. unfaithful leaders\nThe text of Book 11 19 records or illustrates: Faithful vs. unfaithful leaders. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nRepentance and restoration\nThe text of Book 11 19 records or illustrates: Repentance and restoration. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to see that he works through history—using leaders, correcting rebellion, and keeping his promises across generations.\n\nWhy:\nHistorical books record outcomes when people follow or abandon God''s command. Notice who leads, what they do, and what the chapter says happened as a result.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 11 18: Compare with Book 11 18, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\n\nBook 11 20\nBook 11 20. Historical narrative showing God''s guidance through victory, failure, and restoration. Notice leadership, prayer, and the consequences of turning from God. Read Book 11 20 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nGod''s sovereignty in history\nThe text of Book 11 20 records or illustrates: God''s sovereignty in history. Re-read those verses