al narrative showing God''s guidance through victory, failure, and restoration. Notice leadership, prayer, and the consequences of turning from God. Read Book 14 28 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nGod''s sovereignty in history\nThe text of Book 14 28 records or illustrates: God''s sovereignty in history. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFaithful vs. unfaithful leaders\nThe text of Book 14 28 records or illustrates: Faithful vs. unfaithful leaders. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nRepentance and restoration\nThe text of Book 14 28 records or illustrates: Repentance and restoration. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to see that he works through history—using leaders, correcting rebellion, and keeping his promises across generations.\n\nWhy:\nHistorical books record outcomes when people follow or abandon God''s command. Notice who leads, what they do, and what the chapter says happened as a result.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 14 27: Compare with Book 14 27, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\n\nBook 14 29\nBook 14 29. Historical narrative showing God''s guidance through victory, failure, and restoration. Notice leadership, prayer, and the consequences of turning from God. Read Book 14 29 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nGod''s sovereignty in history\nThe text of Book 14 29 records or illustrates: God''s sovereignty in history. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nFaithful vs. unfaithful leaders\nThe text of Book 14 29 records or illustrates: Faithful vs. unfaithful leaders. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nRepentance and restoration\nThe text of Book 14 29 records or illustrates: Repentance and restoration. Re-read t