ctical daily application. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 18 8: Compare with Book 18 8, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1:7: Wisdom books often repeat the theme that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Proverbs 1:7). Compare how each psalm or proverb applies that foundation differently.\n\nBook 18 10\nBook 18 10. Wisdom literature for worship, prayer, and practical living. Apply its truths to your relationships, work, and inner life. Read Book 18 10 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nFear of the Lord\nThe text of Book 18 10 records or illustrates: Fear of the Lord. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHonest self-reflection\nThe text of Book 18 10 records or illustrates: Honest self-reflection. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nPractical daily application\nThe text of Book 18 10 records or illustrates: Practical daily application. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 18 9: Compare with Book 18 9, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1: