heme that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Proverbs 1:7). Compare how each psalm or proverb applies that foundation differently."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Spreuke 1:7","comparison":"Wysheidsboeke herhaal dat die vrees vir die Here die begin van wysheid is (Spr 1:7). Vergelyk hoe elke psalm of spreuk dit toepas."}],"highlights_en":["Practical wisdom for daily choices","Fear of the Lord as foundation","One principle to practice today"],"highlights_af":["Praktiese wysheid vir daaglikse keuses","Vrees vir die Here as fondament","Een beginsel om vandag te oefen"]}],"commentary_en":"Old Testament narrative and teaching. Use the chapter summaries to track God''s covenant story across history.\n\nPsalms 147\nPsalm 147 expresses worship, lament, or wisdom before God. Read it as prayer — many psalms foreshadow Christ and speak to every season of the soul. Read Psalms 147 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nPray the psalm back to God\nThe text of Psalms 147 records or illustrates: Pray the psalm back to God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nNotice metaphors for God\nThe text of Psalms 147 records or illustrates: Notice metaphors for God. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nApply its emotion honestly\nThe text of Psalms 147 records or illustrates: Apply its emotion honestly. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that true wisdom begins with reverence for him and shapes daily choices, speech, and worship.\n\nWhy:\nWisdom passages tie understanding to the fear of the Lord and practical living. Notice commands, prayers, or counsel in this chapter and what attitude toward God they assume.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nPsalms 146: Compare with Psalms 146, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nProverbs 1:7: Wisdom books of