ment against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nSin and repentance\nThe text of Book 30 1 records or illustrates: Sin and repentance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s holy standards\nThe text of Book 30 1 records or illustrates: God''s holy standards. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHope for the future\nThe text of Book 30 1 records or illustrates: Hope for the future. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he calls them back to faithfulness, warns of judgment, and often pairs correction with hope.\n\nWhy:\nProphetic chapters usually contain God''s speech—accusation, command, or promise. Identify what God says he will do and what he asks his people to do.\n\nBook 30 2\nBook 30 2. Prophetic word calling God''s people to covenant faithfulness. Listen for judgment, hope, and promises pointing to Messiah and restoration. Read Book 30 2 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nSin and repentance\nThe text of Book 30 2 records or illustrates: Sin and repentance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s holy standards\nThe text of Book 30 2 records or illustrates: God''s holy standards. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHope for the future\nThe text of Book 30 2 records or illustrates: Hope for the future. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he calls them back to faithfulness, warns of judgment, and often pairs correction with hope.\n\nWhy:\nProphetic chapters usually contain God''s speech—accusation, command, or promise. Identify what God says he will do and what he asks his people to do.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 30 1: Compare with Book 30 1, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\n\nBook 30 3\nBook 30 3. Prophetic word calling God''s people to covenant faithfulness. Listen for judgment, hope, and promises pointing to Messiah and restoration. Read Book 30 3 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nSin and repentance\nThe text of Book 30 3 records or illustrates: Sin and repentance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s holy standards\nThe text of Book 30 3 records or illustrates: God''s holy standards. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHope for the future\nThe text of Book 30 3 records or illustrates: Hope for the future. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he calls them back to faithfulness, warns of judgment, and often pairs correction with hope.\n\nWhy:\nProphetic chapters usually contain God''s speech—accusation, command, or promise. Identify what God says he will do and what he asks his people to do.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nBook 30 2: Compare with Book 30 2, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\n\nBook 30 4\nBook 30 4. Prophetic word calling God''s people to covenant faithfulness. Listen for judgment, hope, and promises pointing to Messiah and restoration. Read Book 30 4 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nSin and repentance\nThe text of Book 30 4 records or illustrates: Sin and repentance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nGod''s holy standards\nThe text of Book 30 4 records or illustrates: God''s holy standards. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHope for the future\nThe text of Book 30 4 records or illustrates: Hope for the future. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that