) VALUES (
    'family-52',
    'Family Devotions',
    'Gesins Toewyding',
    '52 weekly readings for families — short, child-friendly Bible stories.',
    '52 weeklikse lesings vir gesinne — kort, kindervriendelike Bybelstories.',
    '52 weekly family devotions with chapter summaries and discussion prompts.',
    '52 weeklikse gesins toewyding met hoofstuk-opsommings en besprekings aanwysings.',
    52,
    'devotional',
    '[{"day":1,"title_en":"God Creates the World","title_af":"God Skep die Wêreld","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":1}],"commentary_intro_en":"Family devotion reading. Read each summary together, then discuss what you learn about God.","commentary_intro_af":"Gesins toewyding leeswerk. Lees elke opsomming saam, bespreek dan wat julle oor God leer.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":1,"heading_en":"Genesis 1","heading_af":"Genesis 1","summary_en":"The chapter records God creating the heavens and the earth (v. 1). The earth is first described as formless and empty with darkness over the deep; the Spirit of God moves over the waters (v. 2). Over six days God speaks and creation follows: light separated from darkness (vv. 3–5); sky separating waters (vv. 6–8); dry land, seas, and vegetation (vv. 9–13); sun, moon, and stars (vv. 14–19); sea creatures and birds (vv. 20–23); livestock, creeping things, wild animals, and mankind — male and female — in his image (vv. 24–27). God calls each stage good and mankind very good (v. 31). The account of seventh-day rest continues at the start of chapter 2 (2:2–3).","summary_af":"Die hoofstuk beskryf dat God die hemel en die aarde geskape het (v. 1). Die aarde is eers formlos en leeg met duisternis oor die watervloed; die Gees van God beweeg oor die waters (v. 2). Oor ses dae spreek God en skepping volg: lig (vv. 3–5); hemelruim (vv. 6–8); droë land, see en plante (vv. 9–13); son, maan en sterre (vv. 14–19); seediere en voëls (vv. 20–23); vee, kruipende diere, wilde diere en die mens — man en vrou — na sy beeld (vv. 24–27). God noem elke stap goed en die mens baie goed (v. 31). Op die sewende dag rus God, seën die dag en heilig dit (2:2–3).","study_points_en":[{"title":"Creation by God''s spoken command","verses":"vv. 3, 6, 9, 11, 14, 20, 24, 26","detail":"The text repeatedly records God saying \"Let there be…\" or \"Let the earth bring forth…\" and the item appearing. Light (v. 3), the expanse (v. 6), gathered seas and dry land (v. 9), plants (v. 11), heavenly lights (v. 14), sea life and birds (v. 20), land animals (v. 24), and humans (v. 26) each follow a divine word."},{"title":"God declares creation \"good\"","verses":"vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31","detail":"After major stages God sees that it is good (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25). On the sixth day he sees all he made and calls it very good (v. 31). The chapter does not define the word \"good\" but repeats the evaluation at each step."},{"title":"Humanity in God''s image, male and female","verses":"vv. 26–28","detail":"God says \"Let us make man in our image, after our likeness\" (v. 26). They are to be fruitful, multiply, fill the earth, and subdue it; they receive dominion over fish, birds, livestock, and the earth (vv. 26–28). Male and female are both created in his image (v. 27)."},{"title":"Seventh-day rest","verses":"vv. 31–2:3","detail":"After six days of making, God rests on the seventh day from all his work (2:2). He blesses the seventh day and makes it holy because he rested (2:3). The chapter ends with this rest, not with more creation."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Skepping deur God se gesproke bevel","verses":"vv. 3, 6, 9, 11, 14, 20, 24, 26","detail":"Die teks beskryf herhaaldelik dat God sê \"Laat daar wees…\" en dit verskyn: lig (v. 3), uitspansel (v. 6), land en see (v. 9), plante (v. 11), hemelliggame (v. 14), seediere en voëls (v. 20), landdiere (v. 24), en mense (v. 26)."},{"title":"God verklaar die skepping \"goed\"","verses":"vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31","detail":"Na groot stadia sien God dat dit goed is (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25). Op die sesde dag sien hy alles en noem dit baie goed (v. 31)."},{"title":"Mens na God se beeld, man en vrou","verses":"vv. 26–28","detail":"God sê: \"Laat ons mens maak na ons beeld\" (v. 26). Hulle moet vrugbaar wees, vermeerder, die aarde vul en onderwerping uitoefen (vv. 26–28). Man en vrou is beide na sy beeld geskape (v. 27)."},{"title":"Rus op die sewende dag","verses":"vv. 31–2:3","detail":"Na ses dae rus God op die sewende dag van al sy werk (2:2). Hy seën die dag en heilig dit omdat Hy gerus het (2:3)."}],"teaching_en":"God wants us to know that he alone is the Creator of all things, that what he made is good, and that human beings occupy a unique place as his image-bearers under his rule.","teaching_why_en":"The chapter records no other source of existence except God''s word (vv. 3–26). He repeatedly evaluates creation as \"good\" (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25) and mankind as \"very good\" (v. 31). Humanity receives dominion—not independence from God (vv. 26–28).","teaching_af":"God wil hê ons moet weet dat Hy alleen die Skepper van alle dinge is, dat wat Hy gemaak het goed is, en dat mense ''n unieke plek inneem as sy beeld-draers onder sy heerskappy.","teaching_why_af":"Die hoofstuk beskryf geen andere bron van bestaan as God se woord nie (vv. 3–26). Hy beoordeel herhaaldelik die skepping as \"goed\" (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25) en die mens as \"baie goed\" (v. 31). Die mens ontvang heerskappy—nie onafhanklikheid van God nie (vv. 26–28).","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"John 1:1–3","comparison":"Genesis 1 records God creating by spoken command over six days. John 1 records that \"all things were made through\" the Word who was with God in the beginning. Both tie creation to God''s word — one as narrative, one as prologue."},{"ref":"Psalm 104:1–9","comparison":"Psalm 104 praises God for stretching out the heavens, setting the earth on its foundations, and bounding the waters — themes that parallel Genesis 1:6–10 where God separates waters and forms dry land."},{"ref":"Exodus 20:11","comparison":"The Sabbath command in Exodus 20:11 points back to Genesis 1, stating that in six days the Lord made heaven, earth, and sea and rested the seventh day — the same six-plus-one pattern Genesis 1 narrates."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Johannes 1:1–3","comparison":"Genesis 1 beskryf God wat deur gesproke bevel oor ses dae skep. Johannes 1 sê dat \"alle dinge deur die Woord\" gemaak is wat in die begin by God was. Beide verbind skepping met God se woord."},{"ref":"Psalm 104:1–9","comparison":"Psalm 104 prys God wat die hemel uitsprei, die aarde op fondamente stel en waters begrens — temas wat ooreenstem met Genesis 1:6–10 waar God waters skei en droë grond vorm."},{"ref":"Exodus 20:11","comparison":"Die Sabbatgebod in Exodus 20:11 verwys terug na Genesis 1 en sê dat die Here in ses dae hemel, aarde en see gemaak het en die sewende dag gerus het — dieselfde ses-plus-een patroon as Genesis 1."}],"highlights_en":["Creation by God''s spoken command","God declares creation \"good\"","Humanity in God''s image, male and female","Seventh-day rest"],"highlights_af":["Skepping deur God se gesproke bevel","God verklaar die skepping \"goed\"","Mens na God se beeld, man en vrou","Rus op die sewende dag"]}],"commentary_en":"Family devotion reading. Read each summary together, then discuss what you learn about God.\n\nGenesis 1\nThe chapter records God creating the heavens and the earth (v. 1). The earth is first described as formless and empty with darkness over the deep; the Spirit of God moves over the waters (v. 2). Over six days God speaks and creation follows: light separated from darkness (vv. 3–5); sky separating waters (vv. 6–8); dry land, seas, and vegetation (vv. 9–13); sun, moon, and stars (vv. 14–19); sea creatures and birds (vv. 20–23); livestock, creeping things, wild animals, and mankind — male and female — in his image (vv. 24–27). God calls each stage good and mankind very good (v. 31). The account of seventh-day rest continues at the start of chapter 2 (2:2–3).\n\nCreation by God''s spoken command [vv. 3, 6, 9, 11, 14, 20, 24, 26]\nThe text repeatedly records God saying \"Let there be…\" or \"Let the earth bring forth…\" and the item appearing. Light (v. 3), the expanse (v. 6), gathered seas and dry land (v. 9), plants (v. 11), heavenly lights (v. 14), sea life and birds (v. 20), land animals (v. 24), and humans (v. 26) each follow a divine word.\n\nGod declares creation \"good\" [vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31]\nAfter major stages God sees that it is good (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25). On the sixth day he sees all he made and calls it very good (v. 31). The chapter does not define the word \"good\" but repeats the evaluation at each step.\n\nHumanity in God''s image, male and female [vv. 26–28]\nGod says \"Let us make man in our image, after our likeness\" (v. 26). They are to be fruitful, multiply, fill the earth, and subdue it; they receive dominion over fish, birds, livestock, and the earth (vv. 26–28). Male and female are both created in his image (v. 27).\n\nSeventh-day rest [vv. 31–2:3]\nAfter six days of making, God rests on the seventh day from all his work (2:2). He blesses the seventh day and makes it holy because he rested (2:3). The chapter ends with this rest, not with more creation.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that he alone is the Creator of all things, that what he made is good, and that human beings occupy a unique place as his image-bearers under his rule.\n\nWhy:\nThe chapter records no other source of existence except God''s word (vv. 3–26). He repeatedly evaluates creation as \"good\" (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25) and mankind as \"very good\" (v. 31). Humanity receives dominion—not independence from God (vv. 26–28).\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nJohn 1:1–3: Genesis 1 records God creating by spoken command over six days. John 1 records that \"all things were made through\" the Word who was with God in the beginning. Both tie creation to God''s word — one as narrative, one as prologue.\nPsalm 104:1–9: Psalm 104 praises God for stretching out the heavens, setting the earth on its foundations, and bounding the waters — themes that parallel Genesis 1:6–10 where God separates waters and forms dry land.\nExodus 20:11: The Sabbath command in Exodus 20:11 points back to Genesis 1, stating that in six days the Lord made heaven, earth, and sea and rested the seventh day — the same six-plus-one pattern Genesis 1 narrates.","commentary_af":"Gesins toewyding leeswerk. Lees elke opsomming saam, bespreek dan wat julle oor God leer.\n\nGenesis 1\nDie hoofstuk beskryf dat God die hemel en die aarde geskape het (v. 1). Die aarde is eers formlos en leeg met duisternis oor die watervloed; die Gees van God beweeg oor die waters (v. 2). Oor ses dae spreek God en skepping volg: lig (vv. 3–5); hemelruim (vv. 6–8); droë land, see en plante (vv. 9–13); son, maan en sterre (vv. 14–19); seediere en voëls (vv. 20–23); vee, kruipende diere, wilde diere en die mens — man en vrou — na sy beeld (vv. 24–27). God noem elke stap goed en die mens baie goed (v. 31). Op die sewende dag rus God, seën die dag en heilig dit (2:2–3).\n\nSkepping deur God se gesproke bevel [vv. 3, 6, 9, 11, 14, 20, 24, 26]\nDie teks beskryf herhaaldelik dat God sê \"Laat daar wees…\" en dit verskyn: lig (v. 3), uitspansel (v. 6), land en see (v. 9), plante (v. 11), hemelliggame (v. 14), seediere en voëls (v. 20), landdiere (v. 24), en mense (v. 26).\n\nGod verklaar die skepping \"goed\" [vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31]\nNa groot stadia sien God dat dit goed is (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25). Op die sesde dag sien hy alles en noem dit baie goed (v. 31).\n\nMens na God se beeld, man en vrou [vv. 26–28]\nGod sê: \"Laat ons mens maak na ons beeld\" (v. 26). Hulle moet vrugbaar wees, vermeerder, die aarde vul en onderwerping uitoefen (vv. 26–28). Man en vrou is beide na sy beeld geskape (v. 27).\n\nRus op die sewende dag [vv. 31–2:3]\nNa ses dae rus God op die sewende dag van al sy werk (2:2). Hy seën die dag en heilig dit omdat Hy gerus het (2:3).\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet weet dat Hy alleen die Skepper van alle dinge is, dat wat Hy gemaak het goed is, en dat mense ''n unieke plek inneem as sy beeld-draers onder sy heerskappy.\n\nWhy:\nDie hoofstuk beskryf geen andere bron van bestaan as God se woord nie (vv. 3–26). Hy beoordeel herhaaldelik die skepping as \"goed\" (vv. 4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25) en die mens as \"baie goed\" (v. 31). Die mens ontvang heerskappy—nie onafhanklikheid van God nie (vv. 26–28).\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nJohannes 1:1–3: Genesis 1 beskryf God wat deur gesproke bevel oor ses dae skep. Johannes 1 sê dat \"alle dinge deur die Woord\" gemaak is wat in die begin by God was. Beide verbind skepping met God se woord.\nPsalm 104:1–9: Psalm 104 prys God wat die hemel uitsprei, die aarde op fondamente stel en waters begrens — temas wat ooreenstem met Genesis 1:6–10 waar God waters skei en droë grond vorm.\nExodus 20:11: Die Sabbatgebod in Exodus 20:11 verwys terug na Genesis 1 en sê dat die Here in ses dae hemel, aarde en see gemaak het en die sewende dag gerus het — dieselfde ses-plus-een patroon as Genesis 1.","questions_en":["What does this passage reveal about God?","What promise, command, or truth stands out to you?","How can you apply this to your life today?"],"questions_af":["Wat openbaar hierdie gedeelte oor God?","Watter belofte, gebod of waarheid val vir jou op?","Hoe kan jy dit vandag in jou lewe toepas?"]},{"day":2,"title_en":"Adam and Eve","title_af":"Adam en Eva","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":2}],"commentary_intro_en":"Family devotion reading. Read each summary together, then discuss what you learn about God.","commentary_intro_af":"Gesins toewyding leeswerk. Lees elke opsomming saam, bespreek dan wat julle oor God leer.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":2,"heading_en":"Genesis 2","heading_af":"Genesis 2","summary_en":"The heavens and earth are finished (v. 1). On the seventh day God rests and blesses the day (vv. 2–3). The narrative shifts to when God forms the man from the dust of the ground and breathes into his nostrils the breath of life (v. 7). God plants Eden in the east and places the man there (v. 8). Two trees are named: the tree of life and the tree of knowledge of good and evil (v. 9). A river flows from Eden to water the garden and divides into four rivers named Pishon, Gihon, Tigris, and Euphrates (vv. 10–14). God commands the man not to eat from the tree of knowledge of good and evil, warning of death (vv. 16–17). Animals are formed and named by the man; no helper is found (vv. 19–20). God builds the woman from the man''s rib; the man calls her \"woman\" and the text states a man leaves father and mother and holds fast to his wife, becoming one flesh (vv. 21–24). They are naked and not ashamed (v. 25).","summary_af":"Die hemel en aarde is voltooi (v. 1). Op die sewende dag rus God (vv. 2–3). Die Here God vorm die man uit stof en blaas lewensasem in sy neus (v. 7). Hy plant Eden in die ooste (v. 8). Twee bome word genoem: lewensboom en boom van kennis (v. 9). ''n Rivier vloei uit Eden en verdeel in vier riviere (vv. 10–14). God verbied die man om van die kennisboom te eet — sterwe sou volg (vv. 16–17). Diere word gevorm en deur die man genoem (vv. 19–20). God bou die vrou uit die man se rib (vv. 21–24). Hulle is naak en nie beskaam nie (v. 25).","study_points_en":[{"title":"Man formed and given breath","verses":"v. 7","detail":"The Lord God forms the man from dust of the ground and breathes into his nostrils the breath of life; the man becomes a living creature. The chapter records both material (dust) and divine breath."},{"title":"Eden and the two named trees","verses":"vv. 8–9, 16–17","detail":"God plants a garden in Eden and puts the man there. The tree of life and the tree of knowledge of good and evil are named. God permits eating from any tree except the tree of knowledge; eating it brings death (vv. 16–17)."},{"title":"Woman formed; one-flesh union stated","verses":"vv. 21–24","detail":"God builds a woman from the man''s rib. The man says she is bone of his bones and flesh of his flesh (v. 23). Verse 24 states a man shall leave father and mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh."},{"title":"Naked without shame","verses":"v. 25","detail":"The chapter ends stating the man and his wife were both naked and were not ashamed — the last recorded condition before chapter 3."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Man gevorm en lewensasem gegee","verses":"v. 7","detail":"Die Here God vorm die man uit stof van die grond en blaas lewensasem in sy neus; die man word ''n lewende wese."},{"title":"Eden en die twee bome","verses":"vv. 8–9, 16–17","detail":"God plant ''n tuin in Eden. Die lewensboom en kennisboom word genoem. Eet van die kennisboom bring die dood (vv. 16–17)."},{"title":"Vrou gevorm; een-vlees unie","verses":"vv. 21–24","detail":"God bou ''n vrou uit die man se rib. V. 24 sê ''n man sal sy vader en moeder verlaat en sy vrou aankleef, en hulle word een vlees."},{"title":"Naak sonder skaamte","verses":"v. 25","detail":"Die hoofstuk eindig: hulle was beide naak en nie beskaam nie — die laaste toestand voor hoofstuk 3."}],"teaching_en":"God wants us to know that life, marriage, and work are gifts placed within boundaries that protect fellowship with him.","teaching_why_en":"God forms the man, gives breath (v. 7), provides Eden (v. 8), sets one clear limit on the tree of knowledge (vv. 16–17), and institutes marriage (vv. 22–24). The man''s work of naming and keeping the garden (vv. 15, 19–20) happens before the fall.","teaching_af":"God wil hê ons moet weet dat lewe, huwelik en werk geskenke is binne grense wat gemeenskap met Hom beskerm.","teaching_why_af":"God vorm die man, gee asem (v. 7), voorsien Eden (v. 8), stel een duidelike grens aan die kennisboom (vv. 16–17), en stel huwelik in (vv. 22–24). Die man se werk om te noem en te bewaar (vv. 15, 19–20) gebeur voor die val.","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"Genesis 1:26–27","comparison":"Genesis 1 gives the wide creation account; Genesis 2 focuses on the man formed from dust (v. 7), Eden planted (v. 8), and the woman formed (v. 22). Both describe humanity''s origin but at different narrative zoom levels."},{"ref":"Revelation 22:1–2","comparison":"Genesis 2 describes a river watering Eden and the tree of life. Revelation 22 again mentions the river of life and the tree of life — language that echoes Eden without repeating Genesis 2''s exact details."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Genesis 1:26–27","comparison":"Genesis 1 gee die breë skeppingsverhaal; Genesis 2 fokus op die man uit stof (v. 7), Eden (v. 8) en die vrou (v. 22). Beide beskryf die mens se oorsprong op verskillende narratiewe vlakke."},{"ref":"Openbaring 22:1–2","comparison":"Genesis 2 beskryf ''n rivier wat Eden besproei en die lewensboom. Openbaring 22 noem weer die rivier van lewe en die lewensboom — taal wat Eden herroep."}],"highlights_en":["Man formed and given breath","Eden and the two named trees","Woman formed; one-flesh union stated","Naked without shame"],"highlights_af":["Man gevorm en lewensasem gegee","Eden en die twee bome","Vrou gevorm; een-vlees unie","Naak sonder skaamte"]}],"commentary_en":"Family devotion reading. Read each summary together, then discuss what you learn about God.\n\nGenesis 2\nThe heavens and earth are finished (v. 1). On the seventh day God rests and blesses the day (vv. 2–3). The narrative shifts to when God forms the man from the dust of the ground and breathes into his nostrils the breath of life (v. 7). God plants Eden in the east and places the man there (v. 8). Two trees are named: the tree of life and the tree of knowledge of good and evil (v. 9). A river flows from Eden to water the garden and divides into four rivers named Pishon, Gihon, Tigris, and Euphrates (vv. 10–14). God commands the man not to eat from the tree of knowledge of good and evil, warning of death (vv. 16–17). Animals are formed and named by the man; no helper is found (vv. 19–20). God builds the woman from the man''s rib; the man calls her \"woman\" and the text states a man leaves father and mother and holds fast to his wife, becoming one flesh (vv. 21–24). They are naked and not ashamed (v. 25).\n\nMan formed and given breath [v. 7]\nThe Lord God forms the man from dust of the ground and breathes into his nostrils the breath of life; the man becomes a living creature. The chapter records both material (dust) and divine breath.\n\nEden and the two named trees [vv. 8–9, 16–17]\nGod plants a garden in Eden and puts the man there. The tree of life and the tree of knowledge of good and evil are named. God permits eating from any tree except the tree of knowledge; eating it brings death (vv. 16–17).\n\nWoman formed; one-flesh union stated [vv. 21–24]\nGod builds a woman from the man''s rib. The man says she is bone of his bones and flesh of his flesh (v. 23). Verse 24 states a man shall leave father and mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh.\n\nNaked without shame [v. 25]\nThe chapter ends stating the man and his wife were both naked and were not ashamed — the last recorded condition before chapter 3.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants us to know that life, marriage, and work are gifts placed within boundaries that protect fellowship with him.\n\nWhy:\nGod forms the man, gives breath (v. 7), provides Eden (v. 8), sets one clear limit on the tree of knowledge (vv. 16–17), and institutes marriage (vv. 22–24). The man''s work of naming and keeping the garden (vv. 15, 19–20) happens before the fall.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 1:26–27: Genesis 1 gives the wide creation account; Genesis 2 focuses on the man formed from dust (v. 7), Eden planted (v. 8), and the woman formed (v. 22). Both describe humanity''s origin but at different narrative zoom levels.\nRevelation 22:1–2: Genesis 2 describes a river watering Eden and the tree of life. Revelation 22 again mentions the river of life and the tree of life — language that echoes Eden without repeating Genesis 2''s exact details.","commentary_af":"Gesins toewyding leeswerk. Lees elke opsomming saam, bespreek dan wat julle oor God leer.\n\nGenesis 2\nDie hemel en aarde is voltooi (v. 1). Op die sewende dag rus God (vv. 2–3). Die Here God vorm die man uit stof en blaas lewensasem in sy neus (v. 7). Hy plant Eden in die ooste (v. 8). Twee bome word genoem: lewensboom en boom van kennis (v. 9). ''n Rivier vloei uit Eden en verdeel in vier riviere (vv. 10–14). God verbied die man om van die kennisboom te eet — sterwe sou volg (vv. 16–17). Diere word gevorm en deur die man genoem (vv. 19–20). God bou die vrou uit die man se rib (vv. 21–24). Hulle is naak en nie beskaam nie (v. 25).\n\nMan gevorm en lewensasem gegee [v. 7]\nDie Here God vorm die man uit stof van die grond en blaas lewensasem in sy neus; die man word ''n lewende wese.\n\nEden en die twee bome [vv. 8–9, 16–17]\nGod plant ''n tuin in Eden. Die lewensboom en kennisboom word genoem. Eet van die kennisboom bring die dood (vv. 16–17).\n\nVrou gevorm; een-vlees unie [vv. 21–24]\nGod bou ''n vrou uit die man se rib. V. 24 sê ''n man sal sy vader en moeder verlaat en sy vrou aankleef, en hulle word een vlees.\n\nNaak sonder skaamte [v. 25]\nDie hoofstuk eindig: hulle was beide naak en nie beskaam nie — die laaste toestand voor hoofstuk 3.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil hê ons moet weet dat lewe, huwelik en werk geskenke is binne grense wat gemeenskap met Hom beskerm.\n\nWhy:\nGod vorm die man, gee asem (v. 7), voorsien Eden (v. 8), stel een duidelike grens aan die kennisboom (vv. 16–17), en stel huwelik in (vv. 22–24). Die man se werk om te noem en te bewaar (vv. 15, 19–20) gebeur voor die val.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 1:26–27: Genesis 1 gee die breë skeppingsverhaal; Genesis 2 fokus op die man uit stof (v. 7), Eden (v. 8) en die vrou (v. 22). Beide beskryf die mens se oorsprong op verskillende narratiewe vlakke.\nOpenbaring 22:1–2: Genesis 2 beskryf ''n rivier wat Eden besproei en die lewensboom. Openbaring 22 noem weer die rivier van lewe en die lewensboom — taal wat Eden herroep.","questions_en":["What does this passage reveal about God?","What promise, command, or truth stands out to you?","How can you apply this to your life today?"],"questions_af":["Wat openbaar hierdie gedeelte oor God?","Watter belofte, gebod of waarheid val vir jou op?","Hoe kan jy dit vandag in jou lewe toepas?"]},{"day":3,"title_en":"Noah and the Ark","title_af":"Noag en die Ark","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":7}],"commentary_intro_en":"Family devotion reading. Read each summary together, then discuss what you learn about God.","commentary_intro_af":"Gesins toewyding leeswerk. Lees elke opsomming saam, bespreek dan wat julle oor God leer.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":7,"heading_en":"Genesis 7","heading_af":"Genesis 7","summary_en":"Waters recede; Noah exits the ark and worships. God establishes the Noahic covenant — never again a flood. The rainbow signs His promise; Noah plants a vineyard. Read Genesis 7 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"Waters sak; Noag gaan uit die ark en aanbid. God stel die Noagiese verbond — nooit weer ''n vloed. Die reënboog teken Sy belofte; Noag plant ''n wingerd. Lees Genesis 7 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Burnt offering — worship after deliverance","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Burnt offering — worship after deliverance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Covenant with all creation","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Covenant with all creation. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Rainbow as sign of mercy","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Rainbow as sign of mercy. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Human responsibility continues after judgment","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Human responsibility continues after judgment. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."}],"study_points_af":[{"title":"Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Verbond met die hele skepping","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Verbond met die hele skepping. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."},{"title":"Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis","verses":"","detail":"Die teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie."}],"teaching_en":"God wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.","teaching_why_en":"Pentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.","teaching_af":"God wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.","teaching_why_af":"Pentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.","cross_references_en":[{"ref":"Genesis 6","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 6, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 30:15–20","comparison":"Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command."}],"cross_references_af":[{"ref":"Genesis 6","comparison":"Compare with Genesis 6, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander."},{"ref":"Deuteronomium 30:15–20","comparison":"Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel."}],"highlights_en":["Burnt offering — worship after deliverance","Covenant with all creation","Rainbow as sign of mercy","Human responsibility continues after judgment"],"highlights_af":["Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding","Verbond met die hele skepping","Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid","Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis"]}],"commentary_en":"Family devotion reading. Read each summary together, then discuss what you learn about God.\n\nGenesis 7\nWaters recede; Noah exits the ark and worships. God establishes the Noahic covenant — never again a flood. The rainbow signs His promise; Noah plants a vineyard. Read Genesis 7 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.\n\nBurnt offering — worship after deliverance\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Burnt offering — worship after deliverance. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nCovenant with all creation\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Covenant with all creation. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nRainbow as sign of mercy\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Rainbow as sign of mercy. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nHuman responsibility continues after judgment\nThe text of Genesis 7 records or illustrates: Human responsibility continues after judgment. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wants his people to know that he initiates relationship by promise and command, and expects trust and obedience in response.\n\nWhy:\nPentateuch chapters typically record what God said and did toward Israel or the patriarchs—not human achievement alone. Look for direct speech from God and the response it calls for in this chapter.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 6: Compare with Genesis 6, which immediately precedes this chapter in the same book — notice what events or themes continue or change.\nDeuteronomy 30:15–20: Like other Pentateuch chapters, this text forms part of Moses'' covenant instruction. Deuteronomy 30 places life and death, blessing and curse, before Israel and calls them to choose obedience to God''s command.","commentary_af":"Gesins toewyding leeswerk. Lees elke opsomming saam, bespreek dan wat julle oor God leer.\n\nGenesis 7\nWaters sak; Noag gaan uit die ark en aanbid. God stel die Noagiese verbond — nooit weer ''n vloed. Die reënboog teken Sy belofte; Noag plant ''n wingerd. Lees Genesis 7 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.\n\nBrandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Brandoffer — aanbidding na bevryding. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nVerbond met die hele skepping\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Verbond met die hele skepping. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nReënboog as teken van barmhartigheid\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Reënboog as teken van barmhartigheid. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nMenslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis\nDie teks van Genesis 7 noem of illustreer: Menslike verantwoordelikheid gaan voort na vonnis. Lees daardie verse weer en skryf presies op wat die Bybel sê — nie wat jy dink nie.\n\nWhat God wants us to know:\nGod wil sy volk laat weet dat Hy verhouding begin met belofte en gebod, en vertroue en gehoorsaamheid verwag.\n\nWhy:\nPentateug-hoofstukke beskryf gewoonlik wat God gesê en gedoen het—kyk vir God se directe spraak en die reaksie wat hierdie hoofstuk van sy volk vra.\n\nSimilar elsewhere:\nGenesis 6: Compare with Genesis 6, wat direct voor hierdie hoofstuk kom — let op wat voortgaan of verander.\nDeuteronomium 30:15–20: Soos ander Pentateug-hoofstukke vorm hierdie teks deel van Moses se verbondsinstruksie. Deut 30 stel lewe en dood, seën en vloek, voor Israel.","questions_en":["What does this passage reveal about God?","What promise, command, or truth stands out to you?","How can you apply this to your life today?"],"questions_af":["Wat openbaar hierdie gedeelte oor God?","Watter belofte, gebod of waarheid val vir jou op?","Hoe kan jy dit vandag in jou lewe toepas?"]},{"day":4,"title_en":"God''s Promise to Noah","title_af":"God Se Belofte aan Noag","passages":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":9}],"commentary_intro_en":"Family devotion reading. Read each summary together, then discuss what you learn about God.","commentary_intro_af":"Gesins toewyding leeswerk. Lees elke opsomming saam, bespreek dan wat julle oor God leer.","commentary_sections":[{"book_id":1,"chapter":9,"heading_en":"Genesis 9","heading_af":"Genesis 9","summary_en":"Humanity unites to build a tower to make a name for themselves. God confuses their language and scatters them. Babel explains diverse languages; pride is judged. Read Genesis 9 yourself and verify each statement against the verses in your Bible version.","summary_af":"Die mensheid verenig om ''n toring te bou vir eie roem. God verwar hul taal en versprei hulle. Babel verduidelik diverse tale; hoogmoed word beoordeel. Lees Genesis 9 self en kontroleer elke stelling teen die verse in jou Bybelweergawe.","study_points_en":[{"title":"Unity apart from God leads to rebellion","verses":"","detail":"The text of Genesis 9 records or illustrates: Unity apart from God leads to rebellion. Re-read those verses and note exactly what Scripture states."},{"title":"Self-glory replaces God''s glory"